OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026", "title": "The Falconer distance conjecture in all dimensions", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "073", "family_title": "The Falconer distance conjecture", "abstract_html": "We resolve the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension. For every integer <i>d</i> ≥ 2, a compact subset of ℝ<sup><i>d</i></sup> with Hausdorff dimension greater than \\(d/2\\) determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 — Falconer distance conjecture", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(E))>0. \\) No equality of Hausdorff and packing dimensions, Ahlfors regularity, product structure, or Fourier decay is assumed.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge 2\\) be an integer, and let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) be compact.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\Delta(E)=\\{|x-y|:x,y\\in E\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm H}E&gt;d/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(E))&gt;0.\n\\]\nNo equality of Hausdorff and packing dimensions, Ahlfors regularity, product structure, or Fourier decay is assumed. No endpoint or pinned conclusion is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with a Frostman probability on \\(E\\) of exponent strictly greater than \\(d/2\\).</p><p>If Proposition \\(5\\)'s exceptional odd-dimensional affine-plane alternative occurs, Lemma \\(5.1\\), as incorporated in that proposition, already gives positive Lebesgue measure for original distances inside the plane.</p><p>Otherwise Proposition \\(5\\) supplies separated original probabilities and decreasing pair filters. Proposition \\(12\\) constructs positive measures \\(\\alpha_N,\\tau_N\\), all carried by the original distance set, with\n\\[\n\\alpha_N\\downarrow\\alpha_\\infty\\ne0,\n\\]\nsummable total-variation approximation error, and summable square roots of Fourier shell norms. Proposition \\(13\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\infty\\ll\\mathcal L^1.\n\\]\nThis establishes the intermediate conclusion stated in original Proposition 11.5: there is a nonzero finite positive absolutely continuous measure carried by \\(\\Delta(E)\\).</p><p>A Lebesgue null set cannot carry such a measure. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(E))&gt;0.\n\\]\nAll distance pushforwards are by \\((x,y)\\mapsto|x-y|\\) in the original \\(\\mathbb R^d\\); auxiliary projections control only directions. The argument includes \\(d=2\\), whose cap preparation is the integer case.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite-energy restriction and projection principles, sequential uniformity principle, regular-class decomposition, and multiplication of local fibre bounds stated in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.4\\) hold.", "statement": "<p>**Foundational measure and finite-tree tools — original Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, 3.1, and 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Measures are finite Borel measures; restrictions are unnormalized unless explicitly stated otherwise.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  I_t(\\mu)=\\iint |x-y|^{-t}\\,d\\mu(x)d\\mu(y),\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat\\mu(\\xi)=\\int e^{-ix\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\mu(x).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A measure satisfies an \\(s\\)-ball bound with loss \\(K\\) down to \\(\\delta\\) if\n  \\[\n  \\mu(B(z,r))\\le Kr^s\\qquad(\\delta\\le r\\le1).\n  \\]\n  Spherical cap bounds use the chordal metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(R=2^N\\). A factor bounded by \\(R^{o(1)}\\) is called at most subpower.</p><p class=\"li\">Dyadic cubes are half-open. Grids may be translated to give zero boundary mass for the finitely many starting measures.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\ne z\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\pi_x(z)=\\frac{z-x}{|z-x|}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite-energy restriction and projection principles, sequential uniformity principle, regular-class decomposition, and multiplication of local fibre bounds stated in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.4\\) hold. These tools require no lower density bound for the original measure.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(1.1\\) supplies finite energies, positive compact restrictions, and generic projections. Lemma \\(1.2\\) converts sequential subpower contradictions into uniform fixed-power assertions. Lemma \\(1.3\\) supplies regular classes and exactly Lipschitz profiles. Lemma \\(1.4\\) multiplies local fibre bounds with a controlled deletion. These are the asserted foundational tools.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 — finite energy and projection", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mu\\) be supported in a bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^d\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mu\\) be supported in a bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^d\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(\\mu(B(z,r))\\le Cr^s\\) for \\(0&lt;r\\le1\\), then \\(I_t(\\mu)&lt;\\infty\\) for every \\(0&lt;t&lt;s\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(I_t(\\mu)&lt;\\infty\\), then, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), a compact restriction of mass at least \\(\\mu(\\mathbb R^d)-\\varepsilon\\) satisfies a \\(t\\)-ball bound with a finite constant.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(0&lt;t&lt;m\\le d\\), \\(I_t(\\mu)&lt;\\infty\\), and \\(P_V\\) is orthogonal projection to a Haar-random \\(m\\)-plane, then\n   \\[\n   I_t((P_V)_\\#\\mu)&lt;\\infty\n   \\]\n   for almost every \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For part 1, split the energy into dyadic distance annuli. The contribution at scale \\(2^{-j}\\) is bounded by a constant times \\(2^{-j(s-t)}\\).</p><p>For part 2, the potential\n\\[\nU_t^\\mu(x)=\\int |x-y|^{-t}\\,d\\mu(y)\n\\]\nis finite for \\(\\mu\\)-almost every \\(x\\). Choose a sufficiently large level \\(M\\), and then a compact subset \\(K\\subset\\{U_t^\\mu\\le M\\}\\), losing at most the prescribed mass. If \\(x\\in K\\cap B(z,r)\\), then\n\\[\n\\mu|_K(B(z,r))\n \\le\\mu(B(x,2r))\n \\le(2r)^tU_t^\\mu(x)\n \\le M(2r)^t.\n\\]</p><p>For part 3, rotational invariance gives\n\\[\n\\int_{G(d,m)}|P_Vz|^{-t}\\,dV\\le C_{d,m,t}|z|^{-t}.\n\\]\nFor \\(m&lt;d\\), the integral near zero reduces to the finite integral\n\\(\\int_0^1r^{m-1-t}\\,dr\\); for \\(m=d\\), projection is the identity. Integrate in the two measure variables and apply Tonelli.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 — sequential uniformity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed background parameters, let \\(\\mathcal C(\\varepsilon,R)\\) be classes increasing in the allowed loss \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), and let \\(\\mathcal G(c,R)\\) be conclusions weakening as \\(c>0\\) decreases.", "statement": "<p>For fixed background parameters, let \\(\\mathcal C(\\varepsilon,R)\\) be classes increasing in the allowed loss \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and let \\(\\mathcal G(c,R)\\) be conclusions weakening as \\(c&gt;0\\) decreases.</p><p>Assume that, for every sequence\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_j\\downarrow0,\\qquad R_j\\to\\infty,\\qquad\nX_j\\in\\mathcal C(\\varepsilon_j,R_j),\n\\]\nthere are a subsequence and a fixed \\(c&gt;0\\) on which \\(X_j\\) satisfies \\(\\mathcal G(c,R_j)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(j\\).</p><p>Then there exist \\(\\varepsilon_0,c_0&gt;0\\) and \\(R_0&lt;\\infty\\) such that every \\(X\\in\\mathcal C(\\varepsilon_0,R)\\), \\(R\\ge R_0\\), satisfies \\(\\mathcal G(c_0,R)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If the conclusion fails, choose \\(R_j\\ge j\\) and \\(X_j\\in\\mathcal C(1/j,R_j)\\) failing \\(\\mathcal G(1/j,R_j)\\). The assumed subsequence satisfies \\(\\mathcal G(c,R_j)\\) for a fixed \\(c&gt;0\\). For large \\(j\\), \\(1/j&lt;c\\), and monotonicity implies \\(\\mathcal G(1/j,R_j)\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 — regular classes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(n\\ge1\\), let \\(\\rho\\) be a probability on a unit dyadic cube in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and put \\(R=2^N\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n\\ge1\\), let \\(\\rho\\) be a probability on a unit dyadic cube in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and put \\(R=2^N\\). For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\) and sufficiently large \\(N\\), there are disjoint sets \\(E_\\alpha\\), each a union of depth-\\(N\\) cubes, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Their number is at most \\(N^{C_n/\\lambda}\\), every retained class has \\(\\rho(E_\\alpha)\\ge R^{-\\lambda}\\), and\n   \\[\n   \\rho\\!\\left(\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\bigcup_\\alpha E_\\alpha\\right)\n   \\le R^{-2}+N^{C_n/\\lambda}R^{-\\lambda}\n   =R^{-\\lambda+o(1)}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For \\(\\rho_\\alpha=\\rho|_{E_\\alpha}/\\rho(E_\\alpha)\\), there is a nondecreasing \\(n\\)-Lipschitz function\n   \\[\n   m_\\alpha:[0,1]\\to[0,n],\\qquad m_\\alpha(0)=0,\n   \\]\n   for which every occupied depth-\\(j\\) cube satisfies\n   \\[\n   R^{-m_\\alpha(j/N)-C_n\\lambda}\n   \\le\\rho_\\alpha(Q)\n   \\le R^{-m_\\alpha(j/N)+C_n\\lambda}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(\\rho(B(x,r))\\le Kr^s\\) for \\(R^{-1}\\le r\\le1\\), \\(0\\le s\\le n\\), \\(K\\ge1\\), then\n   \\[\n   m_\\alpha(u)\\ge su-C_n\\lambda-\\log_RK.\n   \\]</p><p>Consequently, for occupied \\(P\\subset Q\\) at depths \\(b\\ge a\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\rho_\\alpha(P)}{\\rho_\\alpha(Q)}\n =R^{-[m_\\alpha(b/N)-m_\\alpha(a/N)]\\pm C_n\\lambda}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Discard depth-\\(N\\) cubes of mass below \\(R^{-n-2}\\), at total cost at most \\(R^{-2}\\). Choose depths\n\\[\n0=j_0&lt;j_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;j_J=N,\\qquad\nj_i-j_{i-1}\\le\\lambda N+1,\\qquad J\\le C/\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>Initially label each remaining leaf by its dyadic mass bin. Work upward through the selected depths. For every existing label, extend it by the dyadic bin of the number of children carrying that label in the relevant parent. At each extension there are \\(O_n(N)\\) possible count bins; hence there are at most \\(N^{C_n/\\lambda}\\) final labels.</p><p>For one final label, every occupied depth-\\(j_{i-1}\\) parent has between \\(b_i\\) and \\(2b_i\\) occupied depth-\\(j_i\\) children, where\n\\[\n1\\le b_i\\le2^{n(j_i-j_{i-1})}.\n\\]\nThese comparisons survive later extensions: subsequent labels are constant on each previously constructed child group, so such a group is either retained or removed as a whole.</p><p>All retained leaf masses lie in one dyadic bin. Counting leaves below selected-depth nodes and normalizing gives\n\\[\n\\rho_\\alpha(Q)=2^{\\pm CJ}\\left(\\prod_{h\\le i}b_h\\right)^{-1}\n\\]\nat depth \\(j_i\\). Define\n\\[\nm_\\alpha(j_i/N)=\\frac1N\\sum_{h\\le i}\\log_2b_h\n\\]\nand interpolate linearly. Its slopes lie in \\([0,n]\\). At intermediate depths, comparison with the preceding selected parent and the next occupied selected descendant costs at most \\(R^{n\\lambda+O_n(1/N)}\\), while \\(2^{O(J)}\\) contributes \\(O(1/(\\lambda N))\\) to the base-\\(R\\) exponent. This proves the mass comparisons.</p><p>Discard final classes of original mass below \\(R^{-\\lambda}\\). Their total mass has the asserted bound. Normalization of a retained class multiplies the original ball bound by at most \\(R^\\lambda\\). Covering a depth-\\(j\\) cube by boundedly many radius-\\(2^{-j}\\) balls and comparing with its lower class-mass estimate proves the profile lower bound. Dividing two mass comparisons proves the conditional comparison.</p><p>If \\(\\lambda=\\lambda_N\\to0\\) sufficiently slowly that\n\\[\n\\lambda_N^2N/\\log N\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nthe class count, comparison losses, and normalization losses are \\(R^{o(1)}\\), and discarded mass tends to zero. Fixed deletion powers are obtained only after fixing a sufficiently small positive regularization parameter.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 — density pruning and multiplication", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\rho\\) be a source probability, \\(\\nu\\) a finite pin measure, and \\( 0\\le a_0<\\cdots<a_J\\le N \\) fixed dyadic depths.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\rho\\) be a source probability, \\(\\nu\\) a finite pin measure, and\n\\[\n0\\le a_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;a_J\\le N\n\\]\nfixed dyadic depths. Assume relevant pin–source distances are between fixed positive constants.</p><p>Let \\(G'\\) be a measurable pair set. For \\(j&lt;J\\), suppose \\(E_j\\supset G'\\) is measurable and \\(f_j\\ge0\\), and every \\((x,z)\\in G'\\), with \\(z\\in Q\\) at depth \\(a_j\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\rho\\{z'\\in Q\\cap(E_j)_x:\n |\\pi_x(z')-\\pi_x(z)|\\le C2^{-a_{j+1}}\\}\n \\le f_j\\rho(Q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) is a sufficiently large fixed constant.</p><p>For every \\(e&gt;0\\), one may delete pair mass at most\n\\[\n(J+1)R^{-e}\\nu(\\mathbb R^n)\n\\]\nand obtain \\(G''\\subset G'\\) such that, for every pin \\(x\\), initial depth-\\(a_0\\) cube \\(Q_0\\), and spherical ball \\(B\\) of radius \\(C_12^{-a_J}\\),\n\\[\n\\rho\\{z\\in Q_0\\cap G''_x:\\pi_x(z)\\in B\\}\n \\le R^{eJ}\\left(\\prod_{j=0}^{J-1}f_j\\right)\\rho(Q_0).\n\\]</p><p>Without lower pin–source separation, the same conclusion holds if the local hypothesis tests perpendicular distance to the full line through \\(x,z\\), at width \\(C2^{-a_{j+1}}\\), provided the sets are bounded and diagonal pairs are removed.</p>", "proof": "<p>At every endpoint depth delete \\((x,z)\\in G'\\) whose source cube \\(Q\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\rho(Q\\cap G'_x)&lt;R^{-e}\\rho(Q).\n\\]\nAt one depth, the deleted source mass at a fixed pin is at most \\(R^{-e}\\); integrate in the pin and sum over depths.</p><p>Fix \\(x,Q_0,B\\). Call an endpoint-depth cube hit if it contains a retained point of \\(Q_0\\) with direction in \\(B\\). In a hit parent \\(Q\\), choose such a witness \\(z_Q\\). If a child \\(P\\) is hit, every \\(z'\\in P\\cap G'_x\\) has\n\\[\n|\\pi_x(z')-\\pi_x(z_Q)|\\le C2^{-a_{j+1}},\n\\]\nby separation, the diameter of \\(P\\), and the final cap width. Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{P\\subset Q\\\\P\\ \\mathrm{hit}}}\\rho(P)\n \\le R^e\\sum_{P\\ \\mathrm{hit}}\\rho(P\\cap G'_x)\n \\le R^ef_j\\rho(Q).\n\\]\nIterate through the endpoint depths. The final retained mass is bounded by the sum of the full masses of final hit cubes.</p><p>For the line-width variant, bounded distances from above imply that hit witnesses lie within \\(O(2^{-a_J})\\) of the target line; every point of a hit child lies within \\(O(2^{-a_{j+1}})\\) of the corresponding witness line. The same argument applies, without a Lipschitz bound for the radial map near the pin.</p><p>All partitions and tests are measurable. The density condition concerns \\(G'\\), before the final deletion; no density assertion about \\(G''\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 4.1 — linear projection gain", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(\\epsilon,c>0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), the following holds.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(k\\ge2\\) and\n  \\[\n  k-1\\le q\\le k-\\tfrac12&lt;t&lt;k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All point sets lie in fixed bounded regions of \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\); directions lie on \\(S^k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a tube-width constant \\(C\\). If \\(q=k-1\\), also fix \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A tube is a bounded-length neighborhood of a line; fixed enlargements of its length are permitted.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(\\epsilon,c&gt;0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), the following holds.</p><p>Let \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) be respectively point and direction subprobabilities satisfying \\(t\\)- and \\(q\\)-ball bounds with loss \\(\\delta^{-\\epsilon}\\) down to \\(\\delta\\). At \\(q=k-1\\), assume additionally\n\\[\n\\nu\\{w:\\operatorname{dist}(w,H)\\le r\\}\n \\le\\delta^{-\\epsilon}r^\\zeta\n \\qquad(\\delta\\le r\\le1)\n\\]\nfor every linear hyperplane \\(H\\).</p><p>If \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\times S^k\\) is a measurable incidence relation and every fibre\n\\[\nE_w=\\{x:(x,w)\\in E\\}\n\\]\nis covered by at most \\(\\delta^{-q-\\epsilon}\\) width-\\(C\\delta\\) tubes parallel to \\(w\\), then\n\\[\n(\\mu\\otimes\\nu)(E)\\le\\delta^c.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Rule out a sequence with subpower input losses, at least subpower incidence mass, and covers of size \\(\\delta^{-q-o(1)}\\). Lemma \\(1.2\\) then gives fixed positive \\(\\epsilon,c\\). Restrict directions to one small projective chart, retaining subpower incidence.</p><p>If \\(q&gt;k-1\\), apply Lemma \\(2.4\\) with \\(h=k-1\\). Choose a slice with subpower incidence and normalize its marginals, at subpower cost. The coordinate laws lie in a plane and have exponents\n\\[\nb=q-h\\in(0,\\tfrac12],\\qquad\nf=t-h\\in(\\tfrac12,1),\\qquad b&lt;f.\n\\]\nThe cover count is \\(\\delta^{-b-o(1)}\\). Lemma \\(2.2\\), with the original sliced points and directions retained as labels, gives a contradiction.</p><p>At \\(q=k-1\\), put \\(h=k-2\\); slices are three-dimensional. Write\n\\[\ng=t-h-\\tfrac32&gt;0,\n\\qquad 0&lt;a&lt;g/4.\n\\]\nParameterize the direction chart by\n\\[\nw(p)=\\frac{(1,p)}{\\sqrt{1+|p|^2}},\\qquad p\\in\\mathbb R^k,\n\\]\nwith normalized chart law \\(\\lambda\\). The directional hyperplane hypothesis becomes the affine-hyperplane hypothesis for \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nh&lt;\\ell&lt;(k-1)^2/k=h+1/k.\n\\]\nApply Lemma \\(2.6\\) with small output loss \\(\\delta^{-e}\\), and also delete chart pairs at distance below \\(\\delta^{b_0}\\). Let \\(E_{\\rm pair}\\) be the union of exceptional pair relations. For\n\\[\nd\\lambda_V(p)=\\delta^{-h}\n \\mathbf1_{\\{|P_{V^\\perp}w(p)|\\le\\delta\\}}\\,d\\lambda(p),\n\\]\nthe two-vector kernel gives, whenever \\((\\lambda\\otimes\\lambda)(E)\\le\\delta^c\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V(\\lambda_V\\otimes\\lambda_V)(E)\n \\le C\\delta^{c-\\theta h}\n       +\\delta^{\\theta(q-h)-o(1)}.\n\\]\nClose pairs have expected slice mass at most\n\\(\\delta^{b_0(q-h)-o(1)}\\).</p><p>For retained anchored pairs \\((p,p'),(p,p'')\\), put\n\\[\n\\alpha=\n\\left|\\frac{p'-p}{|p'-p|}\n \\wedge\\frac{p''-p}{|p''-p|}\\right|.\n\\]\nIn the bounded chart,\n\\[\n|w(p)\\wedge w(p')\\wedge w(p'')|\n \\asymp |p'-p|\\,|p''-p|\\,\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n|(1,p)\\wedge(0,u)\\wedge(0,v)|^2\n =|u\\wedge v|^2+|p\\wedge u\\wedge v|^2\n\\]\nproves this comparison. A wedge below \\(2\\delta^a\\) implies\n\\(\\alpha\\le C\\delta^{a-2b_0}\\). The three-vector Haar estimate bounds normalized slicing weight by\n\\[\nC\\delta^{-3hb_0}(\\alpha+\\delta)^{-h}.\n\\]\nThe weak radial cap estimate therefore gives, for the retained small-wedge triples \\(T_\\delta\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V\\lambda_V^{\\otimes3}(T_\\delta)\n \\le C\\delta^{(a-2b_0)(\\ell-h)-e-3hb_0}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(b_0\\) and then \\(e\\) small so this is power-small. With the weak-radial deletion exponent fixed, choose \\(\\theta\\) small so exceptional pair slice mass is power-small.</p><p>Perform the restrictions from Lemma \\(2.4\\), and let \\(\\widetilde\\lambda_V\\) be the restricted chart-label law. For\n\\[\nF(V,z,x)=\\widetilde\\lambda_V\n \\{p:(x,w(p))\\in\\widetilde E_{V,z}\\},\n\\]\nHölder and the subpower incidence lower bound give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V\\int\\!\\int F(V,z,x)^3\n \\,d\\widetilde\\mu_{V,z}(x)\\,dz\\ge\\delta^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe uniform direction-slice mass bound and\n\\(\\int\\|\\mu_{V,z}\\|\\,dz\\le C\\) make the contributions of \\(T_\\delta\\) and \\(E_{\\rm pair}\\) power-small. Thus a subpower amount of triple incidence has original wedge at least \\(2\\delta^a\\). Projection changes each direction by \\(O(\\delta)\\), so the determinant in the three-dimensional slice is at least \\(\\delta^a\\).</p><p>Consequently some slice and transverse triple have a common incident point set \\(A\\) of at least subpower sliced mass. Its point ball bound gives\n\\[\n|A|_\\delta\\ge\\delta^{-(t-h)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nEach of the three original direction labels supplies its own cover by\n\\(\\delta^{-1-o(1)}\\) tubes. Lemma \\(2.5\\) gives\n\\[\n|A|_\\delta\\le\\delta^{-3/2-3a-o(1)},\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(3a&lt;t-h-3/2\\). For \\(k=2\\), \\(h=0\\), and the same proof uses unit slicing weights and the singleton \\(z\\)-space.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Theorem 4.2 — planar discretized Furstenberg input", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a bounded set \\(P\\) of points or affine-line parameters, let \\(|P|_\\delta\\) denote its covering number by radius-\\(\\delta\\) balls.", "statement": "<p>For a bounded set \\(P\\) of points or affine-line parameters, let \\(|P|_\\delta\\) denote its covering number by radius-\\(\\delta\\) balls. A nonempty \\(P\\) is a \\((\\delta,s,K)\\)-set if\n\\[\n|P\\cap B(a,r)|_\\delta\\le Kr^s|P|_\\delta\n\\qquad(\\delta\\le r\\le1).\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;b&lt;1\\) and \\(b&lt;f&lt;2\\). There exists \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\) such that, for small \\(\\delta\\), if \\(P\\) is a bounded nonempty \\((\\delta,f,\\delta^{-\\epsilon_0})\\)-set and every \\(x\\in P\\) has a nonempty family \\(\\mathcal T_x\\) of incident \\(\\delta\\)-tubes whose line parameters form a \\((\\delta,b,\\delta^{-\\epsilon_0})\\)-set, then\n\\[\n\\left|\\bigcup_{x\\in P}\\mathcal T_x\\right|_\\delta\n \\ge\\delta^{-2b-\\epsilon_0}.\n\\]</p><p>Line-parameter distance may be measured by the distance between the associated projection operators plus the distance between closest-to-origin points.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the external tube formulation invoked in the paper: Orponen–Shmerkin–Wang, Definitions 2.5–2.6 and Theorem 2.7, following Orponen–Shmerkin, Theorem 1.3 and Remark 1.4. Decreasing the exponent absorbs fixed charts and fixed width multiples. The range \\(f&lt;1\\) used below is included.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 — planar measure incidence obstruction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<b<1\\) and \\(b<f<2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;b&lt;1\\) and \\(b&lt;f&lt;2\\). There is no sequence \\(\\delta\\to0\\) with the following data:</p><p class=\"li\">Subprobabilities \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) on Borel label sets \\(X,W\\), with measurable coordinate maps\n  \\[\n  p:X\\to\\mathbb R^2,\\qquad v:W\\to S^1\n  \\]\n  having fixed bounded images.</p><p class=\"li\">The coordinate laws \\(p_\\#\\mu,v_\\#\\nu\\) satisfy \\(f\\)- and \\(b\\)-ball bounds with loss \\(\\delta^{-o(1)}\\) down to \\(\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A measurable relation \\(E\\subset X\\times W\\) has\n  \\[\n  (\\mu\\otimes\\nu)(E)\\ge\\delta^{o(1)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every original direction label \\(\\omega\\), the coordinates \\(p(E_\\omega)\\) are covered by at most \\(\\delta^{-b-o(1)}\\) width-\\(C\\delta\\) strips parallel to \\(v(\\omega)\\).</p><p>Distinct labels may have identical coordinates and different incidence fibres.</p>", "proof": "<p>Restrict to fixed bounded charts and normalize, at subpower cost. Let \\(I\\) be incidence mass, and sample \\(n=\\lceil\\delta^{-b}\\rceil\\) independent direction labels. With arbitrarily power-small failure probability, their coordinates satisfy\n\\[\n\\#\\{i:v_i\\in B(v_0,r)\\}\\le Tnr^b\n\\]\nfor a subpower \\(T\\to\\infty\\). Indeed, use finitely many dyadic grids, the binomial estimate\n\\[\n\\Pr(Y\\ge j)\\le(e\\mathbb EY/j)^j,\n\\]\nand a union bound, taking \\(T\\) larger than a fixed multiple of the input loss and \\(\\log(1/\\delta)\\).</p><p>Choose a sample satisfying this estimate and empirical incidence at least \\(I/2\\). The point labels incident to at least \\(In/4\\) sampled direction labels have \\(\\mu\\)-mass at least \\(I/4\\). Normalize their restriction and sample \\(m=\\lceil\\delta^{-f}\\rceil\\) point labels. The same argument gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{j:x_j\\in B(x,r)\\}\\le T'mr^f,\\qquad T'=\\delta^{-o(1)}.\n\\]\nMultiplicity in a \\(\\delta\\)-ball is subpower. Color a spatial \\(\\delta\\)-grid with finitely many colors, retain a color with the most occupied cells, and choose one sampled label in each. The resulting separated coordinate set \\(P\\) has\n\\[\n|P|_\\delta\\ge m\\delta^{o(1)}\n\\]\nand is a \\((\\delta,f,\\delta^{-o(1)})\\)-set.</p><p>For each chosen point label, use lines through its coordinate in its listed good sampled directions. Their tube family satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_x|_\\delta\\ge cIn/T,\\qquad\n|\\mathcal T_x\\cap B(\\ell,r)|_\\delta\\le CTnr^b,\n\\]\nso its normalized loss is \\(\\delta^{-o(1)}\\).</p><p>For each original sampled direction label, moving a prescribed strip axis through an incident sampled point changes its intercept by \\(O(\\delta)\\). Each prescribed strip therefore accounts for only boundedly many \\(\\delta\\)-cells in line-parameter space. The total union has covering number at most\n\\[\nn\\delta^{-b-o(1)}=\\delta^{-2b-o(1)},\n\\]\ncontradicting Lemma \\(2.1\\). Covers remain attached to original labels; coincident projected directions are never assigned one common cover.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4 — Haar band estimates", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(0\\le h\\le n-1\\), and let \\(V\\) be a Haar-random codimension-\\(h\\) linear subspace of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(0\\le h\\le n-1\\), and let \\(V\\) be a Haar-random codimension-\\(h\\) linear subspace of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\). For every unit vector \\(w\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{\\operatorname{dist}(w,V)\\le\\delta\\}\\asymp\\delta^h.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(j\\le n-h\\), and unit vectors \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_j\\) have singular values \\(d_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge d_j\\ge0\\), then\n\\[\n\\delta^{-jh}\\Pr\\{\\operatorname{dist}(w_i,V)\\le\\delta\\ \\forall i\\}\n \\le C_{n,j}\\prod_{i=1}^j(d_i+\\delta)^{-h}.\n\\]\nFor \\(h=0\\), all probabilities and exponent-zero factors are one.</p>", "proof": "<p>The one-vector estimate follows from spherical coordinates. Singular-value decomposition reduces the multiple band conditions to bounds \\(C_j\\delta/d_i\\) on projections of orthonormal singular directions.</p><p>Keep only bounds smaller than a sufficiently small dimensional constant and reveal a random orthonormal frame successively. Conditional on earlier constrained vectors, projection onto \\(V^\\perp\\), restricted to their orthogonal complement, has rank \\(h\\) and nonzero singular values bounded below: the earlier squared projection lengths sum to less than \\(1/2\\). The next spherical probability is at most \\(C_nr^h\\). Since \\(n-j+1\\ge h+1\\), this also covers the limiting one-dimensional kernel. Multiplication gives\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{\\text{all bands}\\}\\le C_{n,j}\\prod_i\\min(1,\\delta/d_i)^h,\n\\]\nwhich is the stated bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.5 — selection of slices", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(0\\le h\\le n-2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(0\\le h\\le n-2\\). Suppose bounded point and direction subprobabilities \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) have \\(t\\)- and \\(q\\)-ball bounds with subpower losses, where \\(t,q&gt;h\\). Directions lie in a fixed small projective chart.</p><p>Let \\(E\\) have at least subpower incidence mass, and suppose every direction fibre is covered by at most \\(\\delta^{-q-o(1)}\\) width-\\(C\\delta\\) tubes.</p><p>For codimension-\\(h\\) subspaces \\(V\\) and \\(z\\in V^\\perp\\), define\n\\[\nd\\nu_V(w)=\\delta^{-h}\\mathbf1_{\\{|P_{V^\\perp}w|\\le\\delta\\}}\\,d\\nu(w),\n\\]\n\\[\nd\\mu_{V,z}(x)=\\delta^{-h}\n \\mathbf1_{\\{|P_{V^\\perp}x-z|\\le\\delta\\}}\\,d\\mu(x).\n\\]\nIntegrate \\(z\\) over a fixed sufficiently large ball.</p><p>There exist measurable restrictions \\(\\widetilde\\nu_V\\le\\nu_V\\), \\(\\widetilde\\mu_{V,z}\\le\\mu_{V,z}\\), and relations \\(\\widetilde E_{V,z}\\subset E\\), inside both bands, such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V\\int\n(\\widetilde\\mu_{V,z}\\otimes\\widetilde\\nu_V)(\\widetilde E_{V,z})\\,dz\n \\ge\\delta^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nTheir masses and ball losses are at most subpower, with exponents \\(t-h,q-h\\). Each remaining direction fibre uses at most\n\\[\n\\delta^{-(q-h)-o(1)}\n\\]\nwidth-\\(C'\\delta\\) tubes.</p><p>These assertions also hold for coordinate maps\n\\[\np_{V,z}(x)=P_Vx,\\qquad\nv_V(w)=P_Vw/|P_Vw|.\n\\]\nThe fibre cover remains attached to each original label \\(w\\). The restriction \\(\\widetilde\\nu_V\\) depends only on \\(V\\) and has a uniform subpower mass upper bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose fibre covers measurably by rounding one axis point of each tube to a finite spatial \\(\\delta\\)-grid while keeping its actual direction. Lists of grid anchors are finite. Select the first candidate covering the fibre up to \\(\\mu\\)-null mass. Parameterized integration makes the selection measurable. For completed-product measurable relations, first use a Borel representative and discard null directions. Slice measures are absolutely continuous with respect to their original marginals, so these replacements preserve sliced incidence.</p><p>Integration in \\(z\\) supplies a factor comparable to \\(\\delta^h\\), and the one-vector Haar estimate supplies another; they cancel the two normalizations. Expected sliced incidence is therefore bounded below by a constant times original incidence.</p><p>Write \\(M(V)=\\|\\nu_V\\|\\), \\(L(V,z)=\\|\\mu_{V,z}\\|\\). Uniformly in \\(V\\),\n\\[\n\\int L(V,z)\\,dz\\le C.\n\\]\nThe two-vector Haar estimate gives\n\\[\n\\delta^{-2h}\\Pr\\{w,w'\\text{ both in the band}\\}\n \\le C(|w-w'|+\\delta)^{-h}.\n\\]\nIntegrating point-band indicators in \\(z\\) first gives the analogous point kernel. Annular summation yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_VM(V)^2+\n \\mathbb E_V\\int L(V,z)^2\\,dz\\le\\delta^{-o(1)},\n\\]\nand, for \\(\\delta\\le r\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V\\iint_{|w-w'|\\le r}d\\nu_V(w)d\\nu_V(w')\n \\le\\delta^{-o(1)}r^{q-h},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_V\\int\\iint_{|x-x'|\\le r}\n d\\mu_{V,z}(x)d\\mu_{V,z}(x')\\,dz\n \\le\\delta^{-o(1)}r^{t-h}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose subpower \\(K\\to\\infty\\) absorbing losses, dyadic sums, constants, and inverse incidence, with retained incidence initially at least \\(K^{-1}\\). Remove planes with \\(M(V)&gt;K^3\\), costing \\(O(K^{-2})\\) incidence. Remove point-slice points violating\n\\[\n\\mu_{V,z}(B(x,Cr))\\le K^6r^{t-h}\n\\]\nat any dyadic radius; their expected mass is \\(O(K^{-5})\\), and incidence cost is \\(O(K^{-2})\\). Remove direction-slice points violating\n\\[\n\\nu_V(B(w,Cr))\\le K^3r^{q-h};\n\\]\ntheir expected mass and integrated incidence cost are \\(O(K^{-2})\\). The resulting point masses are \\(O(K^6)\\).</p><p>Let \\(J(w)\\) be the selected anchor list. In the direction band, a covering tube through \\(a\\) can meet the point band only if\n\\[\n|P_{V^\\perp}a-z|\\le C'\\delta.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nN(V,z,w)=\\sum_{a\\in J(w)}\n \\mathbf1_{\\{|P_{V^\\perp}a-z|\\le C'\\delta\\}}\n\\]\ndominates the active tube count and satisfies\n\\[\n\\int N(V,z,w)\\,dz\\le K\\delta^{h-q}.\n\\]\nDelete incidences with \\(N&gt;K^{12}\\delta^{h-q}\\). Their \\(z\\)-volume is at most \\(K^{-11}\\); the point and direction mass bounds make the incidence cost \\(O(K^{-2})\\). Positive subpower incidence survives.</p><p>On either band, the preimage of a coordinate ball of radius \\(r\\ge\\delta\\) lies in an original ball of radius \\(Cr\\). Projection changes directions by \\(O(\\delta)\\), and active tubes become width-\\(C'\\delta\\) tubes parallel to \\(v_V(w)\\). This proves all coordinate and label assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.6 — transverse tube counting", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\) be unit vectors in \\(\\mathbb R^m\\), \\(m\\ge2\\), with \\( |\\det(v_1,\\ldots,v_m)|\\ge\\Delta>0. \\) If a bounded set \\(A\\) is covered, for each \\(i\\), by \\(M_i\\) width-\\(C\\delta\\) tubes parallel to …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\) be unit vectors in \\(\\mathbb R^m\\), \\(m\\ge2\\), with\n\\[\n|\\det(v_1,\\ldots,v_m)|\\ge\\Delta&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf a bounded set \\(A\\) is covered, for each \\(i\\), by \\(M_i\\) width-\\(C\\delta\\) tubes parallel to \\(v_i\\), then\n\\[\n|A|_{C_m\\delta}\n \\le C_{m,C}\\Delta^{-m}\\prod_{i=1}^mM_i^{1/(m-1)}.\n\\]\nChanging the fixed covering-radius constant changes only the multiplicative constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(L\\) have columns \\(v_i\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|L\\|\\le C_m,\\qquad \\|L^{-1}\\|\\le C_m\\Delta^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\subset\\mathbb Z^m\\) index the \\(\\delta\\)-grid cubes meeting \\(L^{-1}A\\). Each transformed tube parallel to the \\(i\\)-th coordinate axis has width at most \\(C_{m,C}\\delta\\Delta^{-1}\\), hence\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat i}S|\n \\le C_{m,C}\\Delta^{-(m-1)}M_i.\n\\]\nThe discrete Loomis–Whitney inequality\n\\[\n|S|^{m-1}\\le\\prod_i|\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat i}S|\n\\]\nproves the claim.</p><p>As in the paper, this inequality follows by taking \\(X\\) uniform on \\(S\\), applying the entropy chain rule to each \\(X_{\\widehat i}\\), and summing\n\\[\nH(X_{\\widehat i})\n \\ge\\sum_{j\\ne i}H(X_j\\mid X_1,\\ldots,X_{j-1}).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.6", "orig": "Lemma 4.7 — weak radial estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(\\zeta>0\\), and \\( k-2<\\ell<\\frac{(k-1)^2}{k}. \\) For every \\(e>0\\), there exist \\(\\epsilon,c>0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), the following holds.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\nk-2&lt;\\ell&lt;\\frac{(k-1)^2}{k}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(e&gt;0\\), there exist \\(\\epsilon,c&gt;0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), the following holds.</p><p>Let \\(\\lambda\\) be a probability in a fixed bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^k\\), with\n\\[\n\\lambda(B(x,r))\\le\\delta^{-\\epsilon}r^{k-1},\\qquad\n\\lambda(H^{(r)})\\le\\delta^{-\\epsilon}r^\\zeta\n\\]\nfor every affine hyperplane \\(H\\) and \\(\\delta\\le r\\le1\\).</p><p>There is a measurable pair set \\(G\\), omitting the diagonal, such that\n\\[\n(\\lambda\\otimes\\lambda)(G^c)\\lesssim\\delta^c,\n\\]\nand every remaining radial fibre satisfies\n\\[\n\\lambda\\{z:(x,z)\\in G,\\ \\pi_x(z)\\in B(\\theta,r)\\}\n \\le\\delta^{-e}r^\\ell\n \\qquad(\\delta\\le r\\le1).\n\\]\nThe same holds in projective direction space by using two antipodal caps.</p>", "proof": "<p>First prove a single-scale assertion at \\(u=R^{-1}=2^{-N}\\), with any fixed\n\\[\n\\ell_1&lt;(k-1)^2/k.\n\\]\nRegularize the source by Lemma \\(1.3\\), with small fixed parameter \\(\\tau\\), and treat one normalized class \\(\\rho\\), retaining pin law \\(\\lambda\\). Its profile satisfies\n\\[\nm(1)\\ge k-1-C_k\\tau-\\epsilon.\n\\]\nFor an occupied parent \\(Q\\) at depth \\(a\\), a depth-\\(b\\) child has conditional mass at most\n\\[\nR^{C_k\\tau}D^{-1},\\qquad\nD=R^{m(b/N)-m(a/N)}.\n\\]</p><p>Divide depths into \\(J\\) almost equal blocks, ignoring the first two. In every later block remove pairs whose pin is within \\(R^{-1/(4J)}\\) of the source-parent center \\(z_Q\\). The total cost is at most\n\\[\nCJ R^{-(k-1)/(4J)+\\epsilon}.\n\\]\nFor surviving pins, freeze direction at\n\\[\nv(x,Q)=\\frac{z_Q-x}{|z_Q-x|}.\n\\]\nIts induced transverse error inside \\(Q\\) is\n\\[\nO(2^{-2a}R^{1/(4J)})=o(2^{-b}).\n\\]</p><p>For small fixed \\(e'&gt;0\\), call a pair troublesome when the full conditional source mass in the width-\\(C2^{-b}\\) line tube through that pair exceeds\n\\[\nF=R^{e'}D^{-(k-1)/k}.\n\\]\nIf \\(F\\ge1\\), there are no troublesome pairs. Otherwise their sources, for one pin, lie in \\(C/F\\) tubes parallel to \\(v(x,Q)\\), by gridding the perpendicular projection.</p><p>Write \\(B_x\\) for the troublesome set, \\(\\rho_Q=\\rho|_Q/\\rho(Q)\\), and\n\\[\nI_Q=\\int\\rho_Q(B_x)\\,d\\lambda(x).\n\\]\nIf \\(I_Q\\ge R^{-c_2}\\), Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\int\\rho_Q\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{i=1}^kB_{x_i}\\right)\n \\,d\\lambda(x_1)\\cdots d\\lambda(x_k)\\ge I_Q^k.\n\\]\nTuples with determinant of frozen directions below \\(R^{-\\eta}\\) have product measure at most\n\\[\nC_kR^{\\epsilon-\\eta\\zeta/(k-1)},\n\\]\nbecause a Gram–Schmidt distance is below \\(R^{-\\eta/(k-1)}\\), placing the corresponding pin near an affine hyperplane through \\(z_Q\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\eta\\) small relative to \\(e'\\), then \\(c_2,\\epsilon\\) so the latter mass is \\(o(R^{-kc_2})\\). Some transverse tuple has common source mass at least \\(\\frac12R^{-kc_2}\\). The conditional child-mass bound requires at least\n\\[\ncDR^{-kc_2-C_k\\tau}\n\\]\nballs at radius comparable to \\(2^{-b}\\). Lemma \\(2.5\\), applied to the \\(k\\) tube covers, gives at most\n\\[\nCDR^{k\\eta-ke'/(k-1)}.\n\\]\nThese contradict\n\\[\nk\\eta+kc_2+C_k\\tau&lt;ke'/(k-1).\n\\]\nThus \\(I_Q\\le R^{-c_2}\\), uniformly in \\(Q\\), and summation with weights \\(\\rho(Q)\\) preserves the deletion power.</p><p>Apply the line-width form of Lemma \\(1.4\\), using an additional density loss \\(R^{e''}\\) per block. Hit children of one final cap satisfy the same local tube test: their retained witnesses differ from the target line by \\(O(2^{-N})\\), while each child has width \\(O(2^{-b})\\). The density condition is imposed before this last deletion.</p><p>Profile increments telescope. The ignored growth is at most \\(2k/J+o(1)\\), and final cap mass is bounded by\n\\[\nR^{J(e'+e'')+o(1)}\nR^{-\\frac{k-1}{k}(k-1-2k/J-C_k\\tau-\\epsilon)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(J\\) large, then \\(e',e''\\) small, then the remaining losses. This gives cap mass at most \\(R^{-\\ell_1}\\), with power-small deletion. Recombine source classes using their original weights.</p><p>Finally choose\n\\[\n\\ell&lt;\\ell_1&lt;(k-1)^2/k,\\qquad 0&lt;e&lt;1,\n\\]\nand apply the single-scale assertion at dyadic \\(u\\in[\\delta,\\delta^{e/(2k)}]\\). Here\n\\[\n\\delta^{-\\epsilon}\\le u^{-2k\\epsilon/e},\n\\]\nso sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon\\) makes all hypotheses uniform. Total deletion is bounded by\n\\[\nC\\log(1/\\delta)\\delta^{ec_1/(2k)}.\n\\]\nIntersect the surviving pair sets. Dyadic comparison and \\(\\ell_1&gt;\\ell\\) give the required smaller-radius estimate; at larger radii, \\(\\delta^{-e}r^\\ell\\ge1\\), so the estimate is trivial.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Corollary 4.8 — small mass of bad directions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix the parameters of Proposition \\(2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix the parameters of Proposition \\(2\\). There exist \\(c_1,\\epsilon_1&gt;0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), the following holds.</p><p>Let \\(\\mu\\) be a bounded point probability with the \\(t\\)-ball bound of loss \\(\\delta^{-\\epsilon_1}\\). Let \\(B_\\delta\\) be the directions for which at most\n\\[\n\\delta^{-q-c_1}\n\\]\nwidth-\\(C\\delta\\) tubes capture \\(\\mu\\)-mass at least \\(\\delta^{c_1}\\).</p><p>There is a Borel set \\(B_\\delta^+\\supset B_\\delta\\), depending only on \\(\\mu\\) and this test, such that\n\\[\n\\nu(B_\\delta^+)\\le\\delta^{c_1}\n\\]\nfor every direction subprobability with \\(q\\)-ball loss \\(\\delta^{-\\epsilon_1}\\) and, at \\(q=k-1\\), the hyperplane bound from Proposition \\(2\\) with the same loss. No normalization on \\(B_\\delta^+\\) or individual fibres is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Enlarge the tube-width constant to \\(C'\\), sufficient for spatial-anchor rounding. Let \\(\\epsilon,c\\) come from Proposition \\(2\\), and choose\n\\[\n\\epsilon_1\\le\\epsilon/2,\\qquad\n0&lt;c_1&lt;\\min(\\epsilon/2,c/3).\n\\]\nOn a finite bounded spatial \\(\\delta\\)-grid, consider all lists \\(J\\) of at most\n\\(\\lfloor\\delta^{-q-c_1}\\rfloor\\) anchors. Set\n\\[\nB_\\delta^+\n =\\left\\{w:\\max_J\\mu\\!\\left(\\bigcup_{a\\in J}T_{C'}(w,a)\\right)\n       \\ge\\delta^{c_1}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nParameterized integration makes this Borel. Rounding original tube axes shows \\(B_\\delta\\subset B_\\delta^+\\).</p><p>Choose the first successful list at each such \\(w\\). Its incidence mass is at least\n\\(\\delta^{c_1}\\nu(B_\\delta^+)\\), and its cover size is at most\n\\(\\delta^{-q-\\epsilon}\\). Proposition \\(2\\) bounds incidence by \\(\\delta^c\\), so\n\\[\n\\nu(B_\\delta^+)\\le\\delta^{c-c_1}\\le\\delta^{c_1}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 5.4 — thin-tube upgrade", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(k-1\\) is available, then \\(k-\\tfrac12\\) is available.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(k-\\tfrac12&lt;s&lt;k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho,\\nu\\) be bounded separated \\(s\\)-Frostman probabilities in \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_N\\) be Borel pair sets. Their two directional measures are\n  \\[\n  (\\pi_x)_\\#(\\rho|_{(G_N)_x}),\n  \\qquad\n  (\\pi_z)_\\#(\\nu|_{(G_N)^z}),\n  \\]\n  always unnormalized.</p><p class=\"li\">An exponent \\(q\\) is **available** (original Definition 5.1) if, for every \\(e&gt;0\\), some \\(c_e&gt;0,C_e&lt;\\infty\\) and Borel \\(B_N\\subset G_N\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  (\\nu\\otimes\\rho)(B_N)\\le C_e2^{-c_eN},\n  \\]\n  and both directional measures of \\(G_N\\setminus B_N\\) give every cap of radius \\(u\\in[2^{-N},1]\\) mass at most \\(2^{eN}u^q\\), at every retained pin after removal of null fibres.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose both directional measures of \\(G_N\\) satisfy uniform plate bounds\n  \\[\n  \\lambda\\{w:\\operatorname{dist}(w,V)\\le u\\}\\le Cu^\\zeta\n  \\]\n  for linear hyperplanes \\(V\\), fixed \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), and \\(2^{-N}\\le u\\le1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(k-1\\) is available, then \\(k-\\tfrac12\\) is available.</p>", "proof": "<p>Availability is downward closed. It is closed under increasing limits within \\([k-1,k-\\tfrac12]\\): for desired loss \\(e\\), use an available exponent within \\(e/2\\) of the supremum with loss \\(e/2\\), and use \\(u\\ge2^{-N}\\).</p><p>If available \\(q&lt;k-\\tfrac12\\) is given, let \\(c,\\epsilon_0\\) be from Lemma \\(4.2\\), and choose\n\\[\nq&lt;q'\\le\\min(q+c/2,k-\\tfrac12).\n\\]\nFor desired loss \\(e\\), choose small \\(e_1&gt;0\\) with \\(2e_1q'&lt;e\\). First use availability with input loss \\(\\epsilon&lt;e_1\\epsilon_0/2\\). For every integer \\(M\\in[e_1N,N]\\), this loss is at most \\(2^{\\epsilon_0M}\\), so Lemma \\(4.2\\) applies at \\(2^{-M}\\) to the same pair set.</p><p>Intersect all outputs. Additional deletion is\n\\[\nC\\sum_{M\\ge e_1N}2^{-cM}=O(2^{-ce_1N}).\n\\]\nAt these radii the output is stronger than \\(2^{eN}u^{q'}\\); at larger radii the desired bound is trivial. Dyadic enlargement uses the remaining slack. Thus \\(q'\\) is available. Applying this argument to the supremum rules out a supremum below \\(k-\\tfrac12\\); closure supplies the endpoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2 — contact interval", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(m:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb R\\) be nondecreasing and Lipschitz, with \\(m(0)=0\\) and \\(m(u)\\ge su\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(m:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb R\\) be nondecreasing and Lipschitz, with \\(m(0)=0\\) and \\(m(u)\\ge su\\). If \\(q&lt;t&lt;s\\), there exist\n\\[\n0&lt;A&lt;B&lt;1,\\qquad B&lt;2A,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nm(w)\\ge qw\\quad(0\\le w\\le A),\n\\]\n\\[\nm(w)-m(A)\\ge t(w-A)\\quad(A\\le w\\le B),\n\\]\n\\[\nm(w)-m(B)\\ge q(w-B)\\quad(B\\le w\\le1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\ng(u)=m(u)-qu,\\qquad F(u)=\\min_{u\\le v\\le1}g(v).\n\\]\nThe function \\(F\\) is nondecreasing and Lipschitz: a minimizer for \\(F(u)\\) either lies to the right of a later argument or can be compared with \\(g\\) there. Moreover,\n\\[\nF(0)=0,\\qquad F(1)\\ge s-q&gt;t-q.\n\\]\nChoose an interior differentiability point \\(A\\) with \\(F'(A)&gt;t-q\\). At that point \\(F(A)=g(A)\\), since a strict gap would force local constancy. Contact points \\(B&gt;A\\) occur arbitrarily close to \\(A\\), since otherwise \\(F\\) would be constant immediately to its right. Choose such a contact point with \\(B&lt;\\min(2A,1)\\) and\n\\[\nF(w)-F(A)\\ge(t-q)(w-A)\\quad(A\\le w\\le B).\n\\]\nUse \\(g\\ge F\\) for the middle bound, the original \\(s\\)-bound for the first, and \\(g(w)\\ge F(B)=g(B)\\) for the last.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.3 — one-scale improvement", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(k\\ge2\\), \\( k-\\tfrac12<s<k,\\qquad k-1\\le q<k-\\tfrac12, \\) and \\(\\zeta>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\\[\nk-\\tfrac12&lt;s&lt;k,\\qquad k-1\\le q&lt;k-\\tfrac12,\n\\]\nand \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\). Fix bounded-support, separation, \\(s\\)-Frostman, and plate-bound constants.</p><p>There exist \\(\\epsilon_0,c&gt;0\\) such that, for sufficiently small dyadic \\(\\delta\\), if \\(\\rho,\\nu\\) have these fixed bounds and a Borel pair set \\(G\\) has both directional measures satisfying\n\\[\n\\lambda(B(w,u))\\le\\delta^{-\\epsilon_0}u^q,\\qquad\n\\lambda\\{w:\\operatorname{dist}(w,V)\\le u\\}\\le Cu^\\zeta\n\\]\nfor \\(\\delta\\le u\\le1\\), then deleting pair mass at most \\(C\\delta^c\\) makes every remaining radius-\\(\\delta\\) cap have mass at most\n\\[\n\\delta^{q+c}\n\\]\nin both orientations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Treat one orientation and regularize only the source. First prove a uniform assertion for one normalized class; its reverse directional laws remain restrictions of the original pin law, so their constants are unchanged by source normalization.</p><p>If this class assertion failed, choose scales \\(R=2^M\\to\\infty\\), comparison and cap losses tending to zero, and counterexamples to gains and deletion exponents tending to zero. Pass to a uniformly convergent subsequence of class profiles. The limit \\(m\\) is nondecreasing, Lipschitz, vanishes at zero, and satisfies \\(m(u)\\ge su\\). All cell comparisons are \\(R^{o(1)}\\) times those of \\(m\\).</p><p>Fix \\(t\\in(k-\\tfrac12,s)\\), and choose \\(A,B\\) by Lemma \\(4.1\\). Use depths \\(0,\\lfloor AM\\rfloor,\\lfloor BM\\rfloor,M\\). For a source cell \\(Q\\) at an interval's initial depth, define\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_{Q,v}(x,z)\n =\\rho_Q\\{z'\\in Q:\n |\\pi_x(z')-\\pi_x(z)|\\le C_{\\rm rad}R^{-v}\\},\n\\qquad \\rho_Q=\\rho|_Q/\\rho(Q).\n\\]\nComparison sources range over the full conditional class law.</p><p>On either outer interval \\([u,v]=[0,A]\\) or \\([B,1]\\),\n\\[\n\\rho_Q(B(z,CR^{-w}))\\le R^{-q(w-u)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nFor fixed witness \\(z\\), a source \\(z'\\) at distance comparable to \\(R^{-w}\\) can satisfy the radial test only when \\(\\pi_z(x)\\) lies in two caps of radius \\(O(R^{-v}/|z-z'|)\\). Integrating over \\(G^z\\), summing distance annuli, and using the reverse cap bound gives\n\\[\n\\int_{G^z}\\mathcal R_{Q,v}(x,z)\\,d\\nu(x)\n \\le R^{-q(v-u)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nMarkov deletes \\(R^{-e'+o(1)}\\) pair mass and leaves\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_{Q,v}(x,z)\\le R^{-q(v-u)+e'}.\n\\]</p><p>For the middle interval put \\(D=R^{-(B-A)}\\). Rescale an \\(A\\)-cell to bounded size. Its full conditional law has a \\(t\\)-ball bound down to \\(D\\), with loss \\(D^{-o(1)}\\). Proposition \\(3\\) supplies a common Borel bad-direction set \\(B_Q\\) and fixed \\(c_1&gt;0\\): outside it no union of at most \\(D^{-q-c_1}\\) permitted width-\\(D\\) tubes captures mass \\(D^{c_1}\\), and, for every \\(z\\in Q\\),\n\\[\n((\\pi_z)_\\#(\\nu|_{G^z}))(B_Q)\\le D^{c_1}.\n\\]\nDelete these pairs, costing \\(O(D^{c_1})\\).</p><p>For each pin still represented in \\(Q\\), a retained witness has good direction. Let \\(w_{x,Q}=\\pi_x(c_Q)\\). Since \\(B&lt;2A\\),\n\\[\nR^{-A}=o(D),\\qquad R^{-2A}=o(R^{-B}),\n\\]\nso good-direction robustness transfers to \\(w_{x,Q}\\). Taylor expansion shows that the middle radial test implies\n\\[\n|P_{w_{x,Q}^\\perp}(z-z')|\\le C R^{-B}.\n\\]\nThe set\n\\[\nH_{x,Q}=\\{z\\in Q:\\mathcal R_{Q,B}(x,z)&gt;D^{q+c_1/2}\\}\n\\]\nis covered by at most \\(CD^{-q-c_1/2}&lt;D^{-q-c_1}\\) permitted tubes, by gridding the perpendicular projection and counting heavy cells. Robustness gives \\(\\rho_Q(H_{x,Q})\\le D^{c_1}\\). Delete these pairs, again at cost \\(O(D^{c_1})\\).</p><p>The three local factors are\n\\[\nf_0=R^{-qA+e'},\\quad\nf_1=R^{-(q+c_1/2)(B-A)},\\quad\nf_2=R^{-q(1-B)+e'}.\n\\]\nApply Lemma \\(1.4\\) to their common surviving pair relation, using full class comparison sources and endpoint density loss \\(R^{e''}\\). Choose\n\\[\n2e'+3e''&lt;\\tfrac14c_1(B-A).\n\\]\nFinal cap mass is at most \\(R^{-q-c_1(B-A)/4}\\), and every deletion is a fixed power. This contradicts the sequence.</p><p>Sequential uniformity fixes sufficiently small comparison and input losses. Choose the regularization parameter accordingly, discard small classes, apply the class assertion, and recombine with original source weights. Repeat in the other orientation and intersect the pair sets. Decrease \\(c\\) to absorb constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 5.10 — starting pair measures", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) be compact, \\(d\\ge2\\), with \\( \\dim_{\\mathrm H}E>S=d/2. \\) Either the classical threshold in Lemma \\(5.1\\) already proves \\(\\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(E))>0\\), or, after a similarity, there are …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) be compact, \\(d\\ge2\\), with\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathrm H}E&gt;S=d/2.\n\\]</p><p>Either the classical threshold in Lemma \\(5.1\\) already proves \\(\\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(E))&gt;0\\), or, after a similarity, there are compact separated \\(E_1,E_2\\subset E\\) and probabilities \\(\\mu_1,\\mu_2\\) supported on them satisfying\n\\[\n\\mu_j(B(x,u))\\le Cu^{S+\\chi}\n\\]\nfor a fixed \\(\\chi&gt;0\\). Their cross directions lie in a fixed arbitrarily small spherical patch.</p><p>For every sufficiently small fixed \\(\\omega&gt;0\\), there are decreasing Borel pair sets \\(\\Gamma_N\\subset E_1\\times E_2\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\[\n   (\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)\\left(\\bigcap_N\\Gamma_N\\right)&gt;0.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(\\Gamma_N\\) is a disjoint union of at most \\(C2^{2d\\omega N}\\) products of spatial Borel sets.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every \\(e&gt;0\\), some \\(c(e)&gt;0,C_e&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(B_N\\subset\\Gamma_N\\) satisfy\n   \\[\n   (\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)(B_N)\\le C_e2^{-c(e)N}.\n   \\]\n   On \\(G_N=\\Gamma_N\\setminus B_N\\), both unnormalized directional fibres satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\mu_2\\{y:(x,y)\\in G_N,\\ \\pi_x(y)\\in B(w,u)\\}\n     \\le2^{eN}u^S,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\mu_1\\{x:(x,y)\\in G_N,\\ \\pi_y(x)\\in B(w,u)\\}\n     \\le2^{eN}u^S\n   \\]\n   for \\(2^{-N}\\le u\\le1\\).</p><p>Auxiliary projections are used only for directions and incidences. Distances remain those of the original space.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an initial Frostman law above \\(S\\), using Lemma \\(5.2\\). In odd dimension \\(d=2k-1\\), if it charges an affine \\(k\\)-plane, its positive normalized restriction still has exponent above\n\\[\nS=k-\\tfrac12\\ge(k+1)/2.\n\\]\nLemma \\(5.1\\), isometrically inside that plane, proves the distance conclusion.</p><p>Otherwise project generically to \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\), preserving energies above \\(S\\) and zero hyperplane mass. In both parities project further, when needed, to \\(\\mathbb R^{h+1}\\), \\(h=\\lfloor S\\rfloor\\). All target dimensions exceed \\(S\\). Apply simultaneous potential restrictions from Lemma \\(5.2\\).</p><p>Choose two support points whose smallest projected images differ; the projected law is nonatomic. Restrict to small positive compact neighborhoods, separated in every required space and with original cross directions in a small patch. Normalize and retain a common ball exponent \\(S+\\chi\\).</p><p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;\\min(\\chi,h,1)\\), apply Lemma \\(5.3\\) in \\(\\mathbb R^{h+1}\\), and pull its compact restrictions back to the original measures.</p><p>If \\(d\\) is even, \\(h=S\\). Take \\(\\Gamma_N=E_1\\times E_2\\), and use Lemma \\(5.4\\), lifting caps to the original space. This includes \\(d=2\\).</p><p>If \\(d=2k-1\\), decrease the exponent of the \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\) laws to some \\(s\\in(k-\\tfrac12,k)\\). Apply Lemma \\(5.5\\) with the prescribed \\(\\omega\\). Integer caps from \\(\\mathbb R^k\\) lift to \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\) and persist under the filters. Thus \\(k-1\\) is available, and Proposition \\(4\\) makes \\(S=k-\\tfrac12\\) available. Lift the caps and exceptional pair sets to the original space.</p><p>Pullback preserves product masses and decreasing intersections. Each auxiliary side has at most \\(C2^{(k+1)\\omega N}\\) finest cells. Their retained cell-pair atoms pull back to disjoint spatial products, numbering at most \\(C2^{2d\\omega N}\\).</p><p>As in original Remark 5.11, fix the Frostman gap and measures first; choose the later parameters \\(\\beta,\\kappa,\\sigma\\), then the coarse cutoff \\(\\omega\\). Only then fix the plate exponent and invoke the upgrade with the desired angular loss. Its deletion exponent need only be positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.5 — classical distance threshold", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\rho\\) be a compactly supported probability in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\ge2\\), with an \\(s\\)-ball bound for some \\(s>(n+1)/2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\rho\\) be a compactly supported probability in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\ge2\\), with an \\(s\\)-ball bound for some \\(s&gt;(n+1)/2\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^1(\\Delta(\\operatorname{supp}\\rho))&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose positive normalized restrictions \\(\\rho_1,\\rho_2\\) to small separated compact patches. Put\n\\[\na=(n-1)/2,\\qquad\n\\tau=D_\\#\\!\\left[D^{-a}(\\rho_1\\otimes\\rho_2)\\right].\n\\]\nThis is a nonzero positive measure. The sphere-kernel estimate\n\\[\n|\\widehat{\\sigma}_{S^{n-1}}(\\xi)|\\lesssim(1+|\\xi|)^{-a}\n\\]\nfollows from stationary phase on the two stationary patches and integration by parts elsewhere. It gives\n\\[\n\\int_{S^{n-1}}|\\widehat\\rho_1(rw)|^2\\,d\\sigma(w)\n \\lesssim r^{-a}I_a(\\rho_1).\n\\]\nA smooth Fourier ball majorant and the \\(s\\)-ball bound give\n\\[\n\\int_{r\\asymp R}\\int_{S^{n-1}}\n |\\widehat\\rho_2(rw)|^2\\,d\\sigma(w)\\,\\frac{dr}{R}\n \\lesssim R^{-n}\\int_{|\\xi|\\lesssim R}|\\widehat\\rho_2(\\xi)|^2\\,d\\xi\n \\lesssim R^{-s}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a smooth angular cutoff equal to one on cross directions and zero near their antipodes. Leading stationary phase gives, up to Fourier-sign choice,\n\\[\n\\widehat\\tau(r)\n =c_n^{-1}r^a\\int\\psi(w)\\widehat\\rho_1(rw)\n           \\overline{\\widehat\\rho_2(rw)}\\,d\\sigma(w)\n   +O(r^{-1}).\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz and \\(2a+1=n\\) yield\n\\[\n\\int_{r\\asymp R}|\\widehat\\tau(r)|^2\\,dr\n \\lesssim R^{(n+1)/2-s}+R^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe dyadic sum converges; negative frequencies are conjugate and low frequencies are bounded. Plancherel gives an \\(L^2\\) density for \\(\\tau\\), proving the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.6 — simultaneous projected Frostman bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If compact \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) has \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm H}E>S=d/2\\), it supports a probability with ball exponent above \\(S\\).", "statement": "<p>If compact \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) has \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm H}E&gt;S=d/2\\), it supports a probability with ball exponent above \\(S\\).</p><p>Finitely many successive generic orthogonal projections to dimensions strictly above \\(S\\) preserve finite energies with exponents above \\(S\\). After a positive compact restriction of the original probability, all these projected laws and the original law have a common ball exponent \\(S+\\chi\\), \\(\\chi&gt;0\\).</p><p>If \\(d=2k-1\\) and the original law charges no affine \\(k\\)-plane, the projection to \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\) may additionally be chosen to charge no affine hyperplane.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(S&lt;s_0&lt;\\dim_{\\mathrm H}E\\) and an \\(s_0\\)-Frostman probability. Fix \\(u&gt;S\\) below \\(s_0\\) and every target dimension. Lemma \\(1.1\\) preserves finite \\(u\\)-energy under almost every successive projection.</p><p>For each projected law \\(\\eta\\), retain a set on which \\(U_u^\\eta\\) is bounded and pull it back to the original space. Each discarded preimage can have arbitrarily small mass. Intersect the finitely many restrictions and take an inner compact subset of positive mass. The potential argument gives the simultaneous ball bounds. Decrease the exponent slightly to one common \\(S+\\chi\\).</p><p>For the additional assertion, almost every \\((k+2)\\)-tuple is affinely independent, by successive sampling and zero mass on spans of dimension at most \\(k\\). Almost every projection to \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\) preserves independence of each fixed independent tuple. Fubini gives this property for almost every tuple for almost every projection. A projected hyperplane of mass \\(a&gt;0\\) would produce dependent tuples of mass at least \\(a^{k+2}\\), a contradiction. Restrictions preserve the property.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.7 — radial \\(L^p\\) estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(h\\ge1\\), \\(0<\\epsilon<\\min(h,1)\\), and let \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) be bounded separated probabilities in \\(\\mathbb R^{h+1}\\) with finite \\(I_{h+\\epsilon}\\)-energies.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(h\\ge1\\), \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;\\min(h,1)\\), and let \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) be bounded separated probabilities in \\(\\mathbb R^{h+1}\\) with finite \\(I_{h+\\epsilon}\\)-energies.</p><p>For some \\(p&gt;1\\), the radial measures \\((\\pi_x)_\\#\\alpha\\) have jointly measurable densities \\(F(x,w)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\int\\int_{S^h}F(x,w)^p\\,d\\sigma(w)d\\beta(x)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe reverse assertion holds. Positive compact restrictions on both sides can make the two radial \\(L^p\\) norms uniformly bounded at every retained pin.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nu=h-\\epsilon,\\qquad\np=1+\\frac{\\epsilon}{4h},\\qquad\nt=h-\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}.\n\\]\nThen \\(1&lt;p&lt;2\\) and \\(up&lt;t&lt;h(2-p)\\).</p><p>Initially let \\(\\alpha\\) be smooth. For \\(w\\in S^h\\), write \\(P_w\\) for projection to \\(w^\\perp\\), \\(f_w\\) for the density of \\((P_w)_\\#\\alpha\\), and \\(\\eta_w=(P_w)_\\#\\beta\\). The radial density obeys\n\\[\nF(x,w)=\\int_0^\\infty\\alpha(x+rw)r^h\\,dr\n \\lesssim f_w(P_wx).\n\\]\nFor \\(g\\ge0\\), \\(\\|g\\|_{L^{p/(p-1)}(\\eta_w)}=1\\), product Hölder gives\n\\[\nI_u(g\\eta_w)\\le I_{up}(\\eta_w)^{1/p}\\lesssim I_t(\\eta_w)^{1/p}.\n\\]\nThe Riesz-energy identity and weighted Fourier Cauchy–Schwarz imply\n\\[\n\\|f_w\\|_{L^p(\\eta_w)}\n \\lesssim I_t(\\eta_w)^{1/(2p)}\n \\left(\\int_{w^\\perp}|\\widehat\\alpha(\\xi)|^2\n          |\\xi|^{h-u}\\,d\\xi\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nPairings may first use bounded smooth truncations.</p><p>Denote the Fourier integral by \\(A(w)\\), and put \\(B(w)=I_t(\\eta_w)\\). Rotational integration gives\n\\[\n\\int A(w)\\,d\\sigma(w)=C I_{h+\\epsilon}(\\alpha).\n\\]\nWith \\(r_0=1/(2-p)\\), the one-vector estimate and \\(tr_0&lt;h\\) give\n\\[\n\\||P_wz|^{-t}\\|_{L^{r_0}(d\\sigma)}\\lesssim|z|^{-t}.\n\\]\nMinkowski yields \\(\\|B\\|_{L^{r_0}}\\lesssim I_t(\\beta)\\). Raising the preceding estimate to \\(p\\) and applying Hölder gives\n\\[\n\\iint F(x,w)^p\\,d\\sigma(w)d\\beta(x)\n \\lesssim I_{h+\\epsilon}(\\alpha)^{p/2}I_t(\\beta)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p><p>For general \\(\\alpha\\), convolve with a positive smooth approximate identity. Separation persists and energies remain uniformly bounded. The joint measures\n\\[\n[(x,y)\\mapsto(x,\\pi_x(y))]_\\#(\\beta\\otimes\\alpha_\\varepsilon)\n\\]\nhave uniformly bounded \\(L^p(\\beta\\otimes\\sigma)\\) densities and converge weakly to the desired joint measure. Duality gives an \\(L^p\\) density; a countable determining family identifies its sections as radial densities almost everywhere.</p><p>Repeat in reverse orientation. Choose positive compact good-pin sets with bounded norms and valid density identities. Restricting sources only decreases positive radial measures; normalization has fixed cost. At every retained pin, use the jointly Borel density version\n\\[\n\\widetilde F(x,w)=\n \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\n \\frac{K_x(B(w,2^{-n}))}{\\sigma(B(w,2^{-n}))},\n\\]\nwhere \\(K_x\\) is its radial kernel. Spherical differentiation identifies this version almost everywhere in \\(w\\). The compact good-pin sets contain the restricted supports, so no new exceptional pins are introduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.8 — integer caps and lifting", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the uniform conclusion of Lemma \\(5.3\\), for every \\(e>0\\), \\(R=2^N\\), one may delete pair mass \\( O(R^{-e(p-1)/2}) \\) so that both remaining directional measures give every cap of radius \\(u\\in[R^{-1},1]\\) mass …", "statement": "<p>Under the uniform conclusion of Lemma \\(5.3\\), for every \\(e&gt;0\\), \\(R=2^N\\), one may delete pair mass\n\\[\nO(R^{-e(p-1)/2})\n\\]\nso that both remaining directional measures give every cap of radius \\(u\\in[R^{-1},1]\\) mass at most \\(R^eu^h\\), for sufficiently large \\(R\\).</p><p>The bound lifts through a fixed orthogonal projection from a higher-dimensional space if both original and projected cross supports are bounded and separated. Fixed constants may be absorbed by using a smaller preliminary \\(e\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(F,H\\) be the two jointly Borel radial densities and set \\(T=R^{e/2}\\). Delete pairs for which\n\\[\nF(x,\\pi_x(y))&gt;T\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nH(y,\\pi_y(x))&gt;T.\n\\]\nTheir product mass is \\(O(T^{1-p})\\), since\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{F&gt;T\\}}F\\le T^{1-p}\\int F^p.\n\\]\nRemaining directional densities are bounded by \\(T\\), so cap volume gives\n\\(CTu^h\\le R^eu^h\\).</p><p>For projection \\(P\\), actual cross directions satisfy \\(|Pw|\\ge c&gt;0\\), by projected separation and original boundedness. The map\n\\[\n\\Phi(w)=Pw/|Pw|\n\\]\nis Lipschitz there and satisfies\n\\[\n\\Phi(\\pi_x(y))=\\pi_{Px}(Py).\n\\]\nThe image of an original cap, intersected with actual directions, lies in a comparable projected cap. Pushforward of the opposite law gives the lifted estimate, without requiring injectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "Lemma 5.9 — coarse exclusion of heavy plates", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(k-\\tfrac12<s<k\\), and let \\(\\rho_1,\\rho_2\\) be bounded separated \\(s\\)-Frostman probabilities in \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(k-\\tfrac12&lt;s&lt;k\\), and let \\(\\rho_1,\\rho_2\\) be bounded separated \\(s\\)-Frostman probabilities in \\(\\mathbb R^{k+1}\\). Suppose \\(\\rho=\\rho_1+\\rho_2\\) gives zero mass to every affine hyperplane.</p><p>For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\omega\\le1\\), there are decreasing Borel sets\n\\[\n\\Gamma_N\\subset\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_1\\times\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_2\n\\]\nwith positive limiting product mass, each a union of pairs of cells of depth at most \\(\\omega N+O(1)\\), such that both directional measures satisfy\n\\[\n\\lambda\\{w:\\operatorname{dist}(w,V)\\le u\\}\n \\le C_\\omega u^\\zeta\n \\qquad(2^{-N}\\le u\\le1)\n\\]\nfor some fixed \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(a=s-(k-1)&gt;1/2\\). Covering bounded portions of affine \\((k-1)\\)-planes by balls gives\n\\[\n\\rho(L_r)\\le Cr^a.\n\\]\nFix small \\(b&gt;0\\); call a hyperplane heavy at width \\(r\\) if \\(\\rho(H_r)\\ge r^b\\).</p><p>The heavy-hyperplane parameter set has an \\(r\\)-net of size\n\\[\nO(r^{-C_0b}).\n\\]\nTo prove this, fix \\(L&gt;2/a\\). Inside a heavy plate choose \\(k+1\\) points successively, each at distance at least \\(r^{Lb}\\) from the preceding affine span. Forbidden mass is at most \\(Cr^{Lab}\\le r^b/2\\). Admissible tuples therefore have product mass at least \\(cr^{(k+1)b}\\) and simplex volume at least \\(c_kr^{kLb}\\). Two width-\\(r\\) hyperplanes containing the same tuple have parameter distance at most \\(Cr^{1-kLb}\\). In the \\((k+1)\\)-dimensional parameter space, an \\(r\\)-separated list has at most \\(Cr^{-k(k+1)Lb}\\) members compatible with one tuple. Integrating tuple incidences gives the asserted net bound, with \\(C_0\\) independent of small \\(b\\). Fixed enlarged net plates contain all original heavy plates and cells meeting them.</p><p>For \\(r_j=2^{-j}\\), delete every pair of depth-\\(j\\) cells both meeting a heavy width-\\(r_j\\) plate; call the deletion \\(E_j\\). With \\(J_N=\\lfloor\\omega N\\rfloor\\), set\n\\[\n\\Gamma_N=(\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_1\\times\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_2)\n \\setminus\\bigcup_{j_0\\le j\\le J_N}E_j.\n\\]</p><p>At one scale \\(r\\), intersections of net plates with normal angle above \\(\\sqrt r\\) lie near an affine \\((k-1)\\)-plane at width \\(O(\\sqrt r)\\). The union of these intersections has \\(\\rho_1\\)-mass at most\n\\[\nCr^{a/2-2C_0b}.\n\\]\nOutside it, all listed plates containing one first endpoint have pairwise normal angle at most \\(\\sqrt r\\), and their bounded portions lie in a single width-\\(C\\sqrt r\\) plate through that endpoint.</p><p>For \\(j&gt;4j_0\\), put \\(j'=\\lfloor j/3\\rfloor\\). The latter plate is contained in a width-\\(2^{-j'}\\) plate. If heavy at that earlier width, its pairs were already deleted by \\(E_{j'}\\). If light, newly lost second endpoints have mass at most \\(2^{-bj'}\\). Thus new product loss is at most\n\\[\nC2^{-j(a/2-2C_0b)}+2^{-b\\lfloor j/3\\rfloor}.\n\\]</p><p>Treat \\(j_0\\le j\\le4j_0\\) together. Their combined net has at most \\(C_b2^{4C_0bj_0}\\) members. The analogous exceptional first-endpoint mass is at most\n\\[\nC_b2^{-(a/2-8C_0b)j_0}.\n\\]\nFor other first endpoints, lost second endpoints lie in one width-\\(C2^{-j_0/2}\\) plate. Zero hyperplane mass implies\n\\[\n\\sup_H\\rho(H_\\varepsilon)\\to0\n\\]\nby compactness of hyperplane parameters. Choose \\(b\\) so\n\\[\n8C_0b&lt;a/4,\\qquad kLb&lt;1/2,\n\\]\nthen \\(j_0\\) large so total loss is below \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Finally, at a pin, directions near a linear hyperplane correspond to sources near an affine hyperplane through that pin. At an available comparable exclusion scale, heavy plates leave no retained pairs and light plates have mass bounded by width to the power \\(b\\). Below the last scale use that scale; above the first scale use a fixed constant. Hence\n\\[\n\\lambda\\{\\operatorname{dist}(w,V)\\le u\\}\n \\le C\\max(u,2^{-J_N})^b\n \\le C_\\omega u^{\\omega b}.\n\\]\nTake \\(\\zeta=\\omega b\\), or a smaller positive exponent.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 6.3 — half-dimensional angular Fourier estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(S=d/2\\), \\(1\\le H\\le R\\), and \\(K,L\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(S=d/2\\), \\(1\\le H\\le R\\), and \\(K,L\\ge1\\). Let \\(\\alpha\\) be a probability in a fixed bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^d\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\alpha(B(x,r))\\le Kr^S\\qquad(H^{-1}\\le r\\le1),\n\\]\nand let \\(\\xi\\) be a spherical subprobability satisfying\n\\[\n\\xi(B(w,r))\\le Lr^S\\qquad(R^{-1}\\le r\\le1).\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\int_{-H}^H\\int_{S^{d-1}}\n |\\widehat\\alpha(sw)|^2\\,d\\xi(w)\\,ds\n \\le CLK(1+\\log H).\n\\]\nThe constant depends only on \\(d\\) and the fixed support-diameter bound. No ball bound below \\(H^{-1}\\) or Fourier decay is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>The interval \\(|s|\\le1\\) costs at most two. For dyadic \\(B\\ge1\\), push\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{B\\le|s|\\le\\min(2B,H)\\}}\\,ds\\,d\\xi(w)\n\\]\nforward under \\((s,w)\\mapsto sw\\), obtaining \\(\\lambda_B\\). It has mass at most \\(2B\\) and is supported in \\(B\\le|v|\\le2B\\). A unit frequency ball has preimage of bounded radial length in at most two caps of radius \\(O(B^{-1})\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\sup_v\\lambda_B(B(v,1))\\le CLB^{-S}.\n\\]</p><p>Translate \\(\\alpha\\) into a fixed ball. A smooth spatial cutoff equal to one on its support, Fourier convolution, and weighted Cauchy–Schwarz give a nonnegative rapidly decreasing kernel \\(\\Phi\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\widehat\\alpha(v)|^2\n \\le C\\int|\\widehat\\alpha(u)|^2\\Phi(v-u)\\,du.\n\\]\nSet \\(W_B=\\Phi*\\lambda_B\\). Summing the unit-ball bound against the decay of \\(\\Phi\\) gives\n\\[\nW_B\\le CLB^{-S}.\n\\]\nOutside \\(|u|\\le4B\\),\n\\[\nW_B(u)\\le C_MB(1+|u|)^{-M}.\n\\]</p><p>A Gaussian majorant gives\n\\[\n\\int_{|u|\\le4B}|\\widehat\\alpha(u)|^2\\,du\n \\le CB^d\\iint e^{-cB^2|z-z'|^2}\\,d\\alpha(z)d\\alpha(z')\n \\le CKB^{d-S}.\n\\]\nFor the last inequality, split into the ball of radius \\(B^{-1}\\) and annuli \\(2^jB^{-1}\\); all radii are at least \\(H^{-1}\\). Above radius one use total mass.</p><p>Consequently the inner frequency region contributes\n\\[\nCLKB^{d-2S}=CLK.\n\\]\nOn the complement use \\(|\\widehat\\alpha|\\le1\\) and \\(M&gt;d+1\\), giving at most a constant. Sum the \\(O(1+\\log H)\\) shells.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The profile estimates, exceptional-direction estimates, smooth buffered masks, product bounds, and descriptor geometry in Lemmas \\(7.1\\)–\\(7.5\\) hold, uniformly over the regular classes.", "statement": "<p>**Profiles, directional tests, masks, and fixed lists — original Section 6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(S=d/2\\), \\(R=2^N\\), and use the separated starting probabilities of Proposition \\(5\\), with exponent \\(S+\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix, in order,\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\beta&lt;\\min(\\chi/10,1/10),\\qquad\n  0&lt;\\kappa\\ll_d\\beta/d,\\qquad\n  0&lt;\\sigma\\ll_{d,\\beta,\\kappa}\\kappa.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Regularize each starting probability by Lemma \\(1.3\\) with parameter \\(\\sigma\\). For a normalized class \\(\\rho\\) and its profile \\(m\\), define\n  \\[\n  M(n)=R^{-m(n/N)},\\quad A_n=m(n/N)-Sn/N,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  I(g,p)=m(p/N)-m(g/N),\\qquad\n  k(g,p)=A_p-\\min_{g\\le n\\le p}A_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A parallel descriptor \\([g,p]\\), \\(g&lt;p\\), has anchor \\(p\\), nominal length \\(p-g\\). A projection descriptor \\([p,t]\\), \\(p&lt;t\\), has anchor \\(p\\), nominal length \\(t-p\\). The type is part of its identity.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  J_0=\\left\\lceil100(1/\\kappa+1/\\sigma)+100\\right\\rceil,\\qquad\n  W_i(n)=(2-i/J_0)2^{-n}R^{500\\sigma}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an occupied anchor cube \\(P\\), define \\(G_{i,\\mathfrak t}(P)\\) as follows (original Definition 6.2). For \\(\\mathfrak t=[g,p]\\), count all occupied depth-\\(p\\) centers in \\(B(c_P,10(d+1)2^{-g})\\) at distance at most \\(W_i(p)\\) from \\(c_P+\\mathbb Rw\\); the direction is good if the count is at most\n  \\[\n  R^{k(g,p)+C_d\\sigma}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(\\mathfrak t=[p,t]\\), the direction is good if\n  \\[\n  (\\rho_P\\otimes\\rho_P)\\{|(z-z')\\cdot w|\\le W_i(t)\\}\n   \\le2^{-(t-p)}R^{A_p-\\min_{p\\le n\\le t}A_n+C_d\\sigma},\n  \\quad \\rho_P=\\rho|_P/\\rho(P).\n  \\]\n  Set good sets empty on unoccupied cubes and outside the root; \\(G_{i,\\mathfrak t}(x)\\) uses the anchor cube containing \\(x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(q_0=\\lceil\\kappa N\\rceil\\). Partition the depths into consecutive blocks of \\(q_0\\) indices and fix a profile-minimizing representative of each block. An interval \\([a,t]\\) is terminal when \\(t-a\\le100q_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonterminal interval, choose \\(p\\) minimizing \\(A\\) among representatives of whole blocks inside \\([a+q_0,t-q_0]\\); put \\(l=\\min(p-a,t-p)\\). This is a prefix choice if \\(2p\\le a+t\\), otherwise a suffix choice.</p><p class=\"li\">The list \\(\\mathcal M(a,t)\\) contains descriptors generated by every nonterminal subinterval \\([a',t']\\subset[a,t]\\): all parallel \\([g,p]\\) with \\(p-l\\le g&lt;p\\), and, for suffix choices, the projection \\([p,t']\\). Remove repetitions within each type. A generating subinterval is an origin of that membership.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The profile estimates, exceptional-direction estimates, smooth buffered masks, product bounds, and descriptor geometry in Lemmas \\(7.1\\)–\\(7.5\\) hold, uniformly over the regular classes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(7.1\\) supplies all profile and conditional-law bounds. Lemma \\(7.2\\), using Proposition \\(6\\), makes every test's exceptional directional mass power-small. Lemmas \\(7.3\\)–\\(7.4\\) provide smooth buffered masks and stable products. Lemma \\(7.5\\) supplies the nested fixed schedule and its origin geometry. Tests continue to use all specified centers or full conditional class laws; subsequent pair restrictions do not change them.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 — profile bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(7\\), \\( A_0=0,\\qquad |A_i-A_j|\\le d|i-j|/N,\\qquad A_n\\ge\\chi n/N-C_d\\sigma. \\) Every occupied depth-\\(n\\) cube has mass \\(M(n)R^{\\pm C_d\\sigma}\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(7\\),\n\\[\nA_0=0,\\qquad |A_i-A_j|\\le d|i-j|/N,\\qquad\nA_n\\ge\\chi n/N-C_d\\sigma.\n\\]\nEvery occupied depth-\\(n\\) cube has mass \\(M(n)R^{\\pm C_d\\sigma}\\). A radius-\\(C2^{-g}\\) ball contains at most\n\\[\nC'R^{I(g,p)+C_d\\sigma}\n\\]\noccupied depth-\\(p\\) centers.</p><p>If \\(P\\) is an occupied depth-\\(p\\) cube and \\(p\\le t\\), then its full conditional probability, rescaled by \\(z\\mapsto2^p(z-c_P)\\), has an \\(S\\)-ball bound down to \\(H^{-1}\\), \\(H=2^{t-p}\\), with loss\n\\[\nK=R^{A_p-\\min_{p\\le n\\le t}A_n+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first assertions follow from the regular profile, whose slopes lie in \\([0,d]\\), and its Frostman lower bound. Fixed starting constants are absorbed in \\(R^{C_d\\sigma}\\).</p><p>A radius-\\(C2^{-g}\\) ball meets boundedly many depth-\\(g\\) cubes. Divide their upper mass bounds by the lower mass of an occupied depth-\\(p\\) cube.</p><p>For \\(p\\le n\\le t\\), a physical radius-\\(2^{-n}\\) ball meets boundedly many depth-\\(n\\) cubes. Divide its mass bound by the actual \\(\\rho(P)\\):\n\\[\nR^{-[m(n/N)-m(p/N)]+C_d\\sigma}\n =2^{-S(n-p)}R^{A_p-A_n+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nDyadic comparison gives the conditional ball bound. No scales below \\(H^{-1}\\) are used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.4 — exceptional directions", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(C_d\\) in the good tests sufficiently large, depending only on \\(d\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(C_d\\) in the good tests sufficiently large, depending only on \\(d\\). If a spherical subprobability \\(\\xi\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\xi(B(w,r))\\le R^\\sigma r^S\n\\qquad(R^{-1}\\le r\\le1),\n\\]\nthen every level-zero test has\n\\[\n\\xi(S^{d-1}\\setminus G_{0,\\mathfrak t}(P))\\le R^{-\\sigma},\n\\]\nuniformly in the class and occupied anchor cube, for sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a parallel test, divide nonanchor centers into annuli at distance comparable to \\(2^{-h}\\), \\(g-O_d(1)\\le h\\le p+O_d(1)\\). A center is counted only in two caps of radius \\(O(2^{-(p-h)}R^{500\\sigma})\\). Their mass is at most\n\\[\nCR^{(1+500S)\\sigma}2^{-S(p-h)},\n\\]\nalso when the stated radius exceeds one, by total mass. Lemma \\(7.1\\) bounds the center count by \\(CR^{I(h,p)+C'_d\\sigma}\\), and\n\\[\nI(h,p)-S(p-h)/N=A_p-A_h\\le k(g,p).\n\\]\nEndpoint annuli outside \\([g,p]\\) change only constants. Expected count is at most\n\\[\nC(N+1)R^{k(g,p)+C''_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nChoose the test constant larger than \\(C''_d+3\\), and apply Markov.</p><p>For a projection test, rescale the anchor cube to unit size, writing its conditional law as \\(\\alpha\\). Put\n\\[\nH=2^{t-p},\\qquad\n\\delta_A=A_p-\\min_{p\\le n\\le t}A_n,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon=2H^{-1}R^{500\\sigma}.\n\\]\nLemma \\(7.1\\) gives \\(S\\)-ball loss \\(R^{\\delta_A+C'_d\\sigma}\\). A fixed multiple of\n\\[\nF_\\varepsilon(u)=\n \\left(\\frac{\\sin(u/(2\\varepsilon))}{u/(2\\varepsilon)}\\right)^2\n\\]\nmajorizes \\(\\mathbf1_{[-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon]}\\). Its Fourier transform is nonnegative, supported in \\([-\\varepsilon^{-1},\\varepsilon^{-1}]\\subset[-H,H]\\), and bounded by \\(C\\varepsilon\\). Fourier inversion, Tonelli, and Proposition \\(6\\) bound mean collision mass by\n\\[\nCH^{-1}(1+\\log H)R^{\\delta_A+(501+C'_d)\\sigma}.\n\\]\nIncrease \\(C_d\\) and use Markov. If the test threshold is at least one, every direction is good.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.5 — masks with directional buffer", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a descriptor of nominal length \\(l'\\), set \\( D_{l'}=\\max(1,2^{l'}R^{-50\\sigma}). \\) For sufficiently large \\(N\\), every \\(0\\le i<J_0\\) admits an antipodally symmetric mask \\( w_{i,\\mathfrak t}(x,w)\\in[0,1], \\) …", "statement": "<p>For a descriptor of nominal length \\(l'\\), set\n\\[\nD_{l'}=\\max(1,2^{l'}R^{-50\\sigma}).\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(N\\), every \\(0\\le i&lt;J_0\\) admits an antipodally symmetric mask\n\\[\nw_{i,\\mathfrak t}(x,w)\\in[0,1],\n\\]\nmeasurable in \\(x\\), smooth in \\(w\\), constant in \\(x\\) on each anchor cube, zero on unoccupied cubes and outside the root, such that\n\\[\nw_{i,\\mathfrak t}=1\\text{ on }G_{i,\\mathfrak t},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{supp}_w w_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\subset G_{i+1,\\mathfrak t},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|T_1\\cdots T_jw_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\|_\\infty\\le C_jD_{l'}^j.\n\\]\nHere the \\(T_j\\) belong to fixed smooth tangent fields spanning the sphere's tangent spaces.</p><p>A fixed \\(cD_{l'}^{-1}\\)-neighborhood of \\(G_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\), including its closure after decreasing \\(c\\), lies in \\(G_{i+1,\\mathfrak t}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Changing direction by at most \\(cD_{l'}^{-1}\\) changes a parallel tube distance by at most\n\\[\nC_dc2^{-p}R^{50\\sigma},\n\\]\nand a projection-test scalar displacement by at most\n\\[\nC_dc2^{-t}R^{50\\sigma}.\n\\]\nThe consecutive width gap at the fine depth \\(n\\) is\n\\[\nJ_0^{-1}2^{-n}R^{500\\sigma}.\n\\]\nFor fixed parameters and large \\(N\\), each perturbation is below half this gap. Every configuration counted at level \\(i+1\\) in the perturbed direction was therefore counted at level \\(i\\) in the original direction. The strict margin also handles closure.</p><p>Take \\(r=c_0D_{l'}^{-1}\\). Smooth the indicator of the spherical \\(2r\\)-neighborhood of \\(G_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\) by a nonnegative rotationally invariant kernel of integral one supported in radius \\(r\\). The convolution equals one on \\(G_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\), is supported in its closed \\(3r\\)-neighborhood, and has derivative bounds \\(C_jr^{-j}\\). Tests and kernel are antipodally symmetric. Construct separately on the finitely many anchor cubes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.6 — products and retained supports", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Consider at most \\(N^{C_*}\\) mask occurrences, with \\(C_*\\) fixed.", "statement": "<p>Consider at most \\(N^{C_*}\\) mask occurrences, with \\(C_*\\) fixed. A factor is either \\(w_{i,\\mathfrak t}\\) or a derivative of order \\(q\\) divided by \\(D_{l'}^q\\). The total prescribed derivative order is bounded independently of \\(N\\); indices may differ. Allow boundedly many measurable spatial multipliers and smooth angular factors, with fixed common numerical bounds.</p><p>If \\(L\\) is the largest nominal length, or zero for an empty list, set\n\\[\nD_*=R^\\sigma\\max(1,2^LR^{-50\\sigma}).\n\\]\nEvery such product \\(b\\) satisfies, at each fixed angular derivative order \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\|T_1\\cdots T_jb\\|_\\infty\\le C_jD_*^j.\n\\]\nEach factor retains its \\(G_{i+1,\\mathfrak t}\\) support condition, including after differentiation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Undifferentiated masks have norm at most one. Only boundedly many factors are prescribed derivatives; their normalized derivatives satisfy the corresponding bounds from Lemma \\(7.3\\). At order \\(j\\), the product rule produces polynomially many terms in \\(N\\), each bounded by\n\\[\nC_j\\max(1,2^LR^{-50\\sigma})^j.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\sigma\\) and large \\(N\\), the polynomial factor is at most \\(R^{\\sigma j}\\). At order zero there is no product-rule multiplicity. Derivatives of a smooth function have support contained in its support, so every imposed condition remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Lemma 6.7 — descriptor geometry", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a nonterminal \\([a,t]\\), the eligible whole blocks form an integer interval \\([a_*,t_*]\\) satisfying \\( a+q_0\\le a_*\\le a+2q_0,\\qquad t-2q_0\\le t_*\\le t-q_0, \\) and the selected anchor satisfies …", "statement": "<p>For a nonterminal \\([a,t]\\), the eligible whole blocks form an integer interval \\([a_*,t_*]\\) satisfying\n\\[\na+q_0\\le a_*\\le a+2q_0,\\qquad\nt-2q_0\\le t_*\\le t-q_0,\n\\]\nand the selected anchor satisfies\n\\[\nA_n\\ge A_p\\qquad(a_*\\le n\\le t_*).\n\\]</p><p>The lists \\(\\mathcal M(a,t)\\) are nested under interval containment. They use \\(O(1/\\kappa)\\) possible anchors and polynomially many descriptors.</p><p>Every membership \\(\\mathfrak t\\in\\mathcal M(a,t)\\), with anchor \\(p\\) and nominal length \\(l'\\), has an origin \\([a',t']\\subset[a,t]\\) satisfying\n\\[\n[p-l',p+l']\\subset[a',t'].\n\\]\nThat origin also supplies all parallel \\([g,p]\\) with \\(p-l'\\le g&lt;p\\). In particular \\(l'\\le(t-a)/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Eligible blocks are consecutive, with endpoints within one extra block of the prescribed inner endpoints. Every depth in their union has profile value at least its own representative's value, and the selected representative has the minimum of those values.</p><p>An origin's choice is made once, independently of the larger list in which it appears; hence lists are nested. There are at most \\(1+(N+1)/q_0\\) anchors. A descriptor is determined by its type, anchor, and one further depth, so the count is polynomial.</p><p>A generated parallel descriptor has length at most \\(l=\\min(p-a',t'-p)\\). A generated projection descriptor occurs only at a suffix choice and has length \\(t'-p=l\\le p-a'\\). Both assertions give the symmetric contained interval and the accompanying parallel descriptors. Deduplication does not remove the existence of an origin for each membership.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 7.4 — weighted shell conversion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(0\\le a\\le v\\le N\\), \\( v-a\\ge20\\sigma N,\\qquad L\\le(v-a)/2+\\sigma N, \\) then, for every fixed \\(T>0\\), \\( 2^{-a}\\int_{2^{v-1}}^{2^{v+1}}|\\widehat\\nu(r)|^2\\,dr \\le R^{o(1)}\\sup H_F(a,v)+O(R^{-T}). \\) The supremum …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use regular class laws \\(\\rho_1,\\rho_2\\), with model masses \\(M_1,M_2\\), and masks from Proposition \\(7\\). Put \\(n_*=d-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a power of two \\(K_d\\) with \\(1000(d+1)&lt;K_d&lt;4000(d+1)\\). Depth-\\(a\\) cells \\(X,Y\\) are separated if\n  \\[\n  K_d2^{-a}/8\\le|c_X-c_Y|\\le4K_d2^{-a}.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(D(x,y)=|x-y|\\asymp_d2^{-a}\\). Put \\(e(x,y)=(x-y)/D(x,y)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(0\\le\\psi\\le1\\) smooth, equal to one near all relevant \\(e(x,y)\\), and zero near their negatives. Fixed outer separated supports use the same convention with \\(a=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Omega_{uv}=w_u\\partial_{w_v}-w_v\\partial_{w_u}.\n  \\]\n  An admissible endpoint symbol (original Definition 7.1) is\n  \\[\n  b_x(w)=f(x)q(w)\n    \\prod_{\\nu\\in I}D_{l_\\nu}^{-|\\alpha_\\nu|}\n       \\Omega^{\\alpha_\\nu}w_{i,\\mathfrak t_\\nu}(x,w),\n  \\]\n  where \\(|f|\\le1\\), \\(\\#I\\le N^{C_A}\\), \\(\\sum_\\nu|\\alpha_\\nu|\\le B\\), and \\(q\\) has fixed common \\(C^j\\) bounds. The index \\(i&lt;J_0\\), exponent \\(C_A\\), derivative budget \\(B\\), and numerical angular bounds are fixed independently of \\(N\\). Repetitions and complex conjugates are allowed; symbols do not depend on radial frequency.</p><p class=\"li\">For specified endpoint symbols define\n  \\[\n  U_X(r,w)=M_1(a)^{-1}\\int_Xe^{irw\\cdot x}b_x(w)\\,d\\rho_1(x),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  V_Y(r,w)=M_2(a)^{-1}\\int_Ye^{irw\\cdot y}c_y(w)\\,d\\rho_2(y).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\Psi\\ge0\\) in \\(C_c^\\infty((1/4,4))\\), \\(\\Psi\\ge1\\) on \\([1/2,2]\\). Define the independent energy (original Definition 7.3)\n  \\[\n  H^{X,Y}(a,v)=2^{d(v-a)}\n   \\int\\Psi(r/2^v)\n    \\left(\\int|U_X(r,w)|^2\\,dw\\right)\n    \\left(\\int|V_Y(r,w)|^2\\,dw\\right)\\frac{dr}{2^v},\n  \\]\n  and, in separated geometry,\n  \\[\n  H_F^{X,Y}(a,v)=2^{d(v-a)}\n   \\int\\Psi(r/2^v)\n    \\left|\\int\\psi(w)U_X(r,w)\\overline{V_Y(r,w)}\\,dw\\right|^2\n    \\frac{dr}{2^v}.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(H_F^{X,Y}\\le H^{X,Y}\\), by angular Cauchy–Schwarz.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m_x,n_y\\) be products of undifferentiated masks at one index \\(i\\), with polynomial-size indexed lists. Let \\(L\\) be their largest nominal length, or zero for empty lists.</p><p class=\"li\">For measurable \\(|f|,|g|\\le1\\), define a possibly complex scalar measure \\(\\nu\\) by\n  \\[\n  \\int F\\,d\\nu=\n   \\frac1{M_1(a)M_2(a)}\n   \\int_{X\\times Y}F(D(x,y))(D(x,y)2^a)^{-n_*/2}\n   f(x)g(y)m_x(e(x,y))n_y(e(x,y))\n   \\,d\\rho_1(x)d\\rho_2(y).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(0\\le a\\le v\\le N\\),\n\\[\nv-a\\ge20\\sigma N,\\qquad L\\le(v-a)/2+\\sigma N,\n\\]\nthen, for every fixed \\(T&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n2^{-a}\\int_{2^{v-1}}^{2^{v+1}}|\\widehat\\nu(r)|^2\\,dr\n \\le R^{o(1)}\\sup H_F(a,v)+O(R^{-T}).\n\\]\nThe supremum retains the cells, class normalizations, descriptor lists, and smoothing index. Its admissible derivative budget and numerical angular bounds depend only on \\(T,d,C_A\\), and fixed parameters, not on \\(N\\). The assertion holds at the fixed outer supports with \\(a=0\\). For \\(f=g=1\\), \\(\\nu\\) is positive.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(q=v-a\\), \\(s=r/2^v\\), and \\(D_{\\max}=\\max(1,2^LR^{-50\\sigma})\\). The hypotheses give\n\\[\nD_*=R^\\sigma D_{\\max}\n \\le\\max(R^\\sigma,2^{q/2}R^{-48\\sigma}),\n\\]\n\\[\nD_*^2/\\Lambda\\lesssim\n \\max(R^{2\\sigma}2^{-q},R^{-96\\sigma})\n \\lesssim R^{-18\\sigma},\n\\qquad \\Lambda\\asymp2^q.\n\\]\nThus Lemma \\(8.2\\) applies uniformly to the endpoint-mask product.</p><p>Estimate \\(\\widehat\\nu(-r)\\), using the outgoing inverse expansion. Conjugating spatial multipliers gives the positive-frequency assertion. The distance weight cancels the leading stationary distance power:\n\\[\n(D2^a)^{-n_*/2}(rD)^{n_*/2}(rD)^{-j}\n =(r2^{-a})^{n_*/2}(r2^{-a})^{-j}(D2^a)^{-j}.\n\\]\nExpand the global \\(B_j\\) by Leibniz. Normalize each differentiated mask by its own derivative scale. The resulting endpoint symbols retain all descriptors and have a fixed derivative budget. Their extracted scales have product at most \\(D_{\\max}^{2j}\\), paid by\n\\[\n2^{-jq}D_{\\max}^{2j}\n \\le[\\max(2^{-q},R^{-98\\sigma})]^j\\le1.\n\\]\nThere are polynomially many terms for fixed \\(j\\).</p><p>Use Lemma \\(8.3\\) to separate the remaining spatial coefficient. Its absolutely summable series only multiplies the allowed bounded spatial factors. Consequently\n\\[\n\\widehat\\nu(-2^vs)\n =2^{n_*q/2}\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(s)\n    \\int\\psi(w)U_{X,\\alpha}(2^vs,w)\n             \\overline{V_{Y,\\alpha}(2^vs,w)}\\,dw\n   +E(2^vs),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\sum_\\alpha\\|c_\\alpha\\|_{L^\\infty([1/2,2])}\\le R^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe error is smaller than any prescribed negative power after increasing the expansion order; this absorbs normalized cell masses \\(R^{O_d(\\sigma)}\\), squaring, and shell length at most \\(2R\\).</p><p>Apply the \\(L^2(ds)\\) triangle inequality. Since \\(\\Psi\\ge1\\) on this interval, the resulting squared angular integrals are bounded by their \\(H_F\\)-energies. The complete normalization is\n\\[\n2^{-a}2^v2^{n_*(v-a)}=2^{d(v-a)}.\n\\]\nThis is exactly the prefactor defining \\(H_F(a,v)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2 — derivative bounds and reached anchors", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an admissible symbol, or a fixed product of such symbols, let \\(L\\) be the largest nominal length and \\( D_*=R^\\sigma\\max(1,2^LR^{-50\\sigma}). \\) Then \\( \\sup_x\\|b_x\\|_{C^j(S^{d-1})}\\le C_{j,B}D_*^j \\) at every …", "statement": "<p>For an admissible symbol, or a fixed product of such symbols, let \\(L\\) be the largest nominal length and\n\\[\nD_*=R^\\sigma\\max(1,2^LR^{-50\\sigma}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sup_x\\|b_x\\|_{C^j(S^{d-1})}\\le C_{j,B}D_*^j\n\\]\nat every fixed order.</p><p>On a depth-\\(a\\) cell, the product of factors anchored at depths at most \\(a\\) is independent of the spatial variable and has bounded modulus. Such anchors are called reached. In a positive independent energy these factors may be removed at bounded cost, after factoring them out of the endpoint transforms. This operation is not asserted inside the shared-angle integral before its absolute square.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma \\(7.4\\) and the finite product rule. A reached anchor cell contains the depth-\\(a\\) cell, so its mask and angular derivatives are spatially constant there. Undifferentiated masks have modulus at most one; only boundedly many prescribed derivative factors occur. Their product has bounded modulus. Bounding its modulus inside a positive angular integral permits removal.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.5 — direct and inverse sphere expansions", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use \\(\\Delta_{S^{n_*}}=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\), and define \\( c_{n_*}=(2\\pi)^{n_*/2}e^{-i\\pi n_*/4},\\qquad L_0=c_{n_*}\\operatorname{Id}, \\) …", "statement": "<p>Use \\(\\Delta_{S^{n_*}}=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\), and define\n\\[\nc_{n_*}=(2\\pi)^{n_*/2}e^{-i\\pi n_*/4},\\qquad L_0=c_{n_*}\\operatorname{Id},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_j=\\frac{c_{n_*}}{(2i)^jj!}\n \\prod_{q=0}^{j-1}\n \\left(\\Delta_{S^{n_*}}+q(q+1)-\\frac{n_*(n_*-2)}4\\right).\n\\]\nSet \\(L_{j,+1}=L_j\\) and \\(L_{j,-1}b=\\overline{L_j\\overline b}\\).</p><p>Let \\(b\\in C^\\infty(S^{n_*})\\) satisfy \\(\\|b\\|_{C^m}\\le C_mA^m\\), \\(A\\ge1\\), at the finite orders required. For \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\), put\n\\[\ne=\\Delta/|\\Delta|,\\qquad \\Lambda=r|\\Delta|,\\qquad r&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Lambda\\ge1\\) and \\(\\psi\\) equals one near \\(e\\) and zero near \\(-e\\), then\n\\[\n\\int e^{irw\\cdot\\Delta}\\psi(w)b(w)\\,dw\n =\\Lambda^{-n_*/2}\n  \\left[e^{i\\Lambda}\\sum_{j&lt;J}\\Lambda^{-j}(L_jb)(e)+E_J\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\n|E_J|\\le C_J\\Lambda^{-J}A^{2J+d+2}.\n\\]\nWithout cutoff, sum the two stationary contributions\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{-n_*/2}\n \\sum_{\\varepsilon=\\pm1}e^{i\\varepsilon\\Lambda}\n \\sum_{j&lt;J}\\Lambda^{-j}(L_{j,\\varepsilon}b)(\\varepsilon e)\n\\]\nwith the same extracted remainder bound.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nB_0=L_0^{-1},\\qquad\nB_j=-L_0^{-1}\\sum_{h=1}^jL_hB_{j-h}.\n\\]\nEach \\(B_j\\) has order at most \\(2j\\). If, for fixed \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Lambda\\ge R^{c\\sigma},\\qquad A^2/\\Lambda\\le R^{-c\\sigma},\n\\qquad1\\le A\\le R^2,\n\\]\nthen, for every fixed \\(T&gt;0\\) and sufficiently large fixed \\(J\\),\n\\[\ne^{i\\Lambda}b(e)\n =\\Lambda^{n_*/2}\\sum_{j&lt;J}\\Lambda^{-j}\n   \\int e^{irw\\cdot\\Delta}\\psi(w)(B_jb)(w)\\,dw\n   +O(R^{-T}).\n\\]\nThe direct extracted remainders are also \\(O(R^{-T})\\).</p><p>For \\(r=2^vs\\), \\(s\\) in a fixed compact subset of \\((0,\\infty)\\), the coefficients have bounded derivatives at every fixed order in \\(s\\). Differential coefficients retain supports of all mask factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from \\(\\pm e\\), integrate repeatedly by parts with the angular gradient of \\(w\\cdot e\\). Near the maximum, rotate \\(e\\) to the final coordinate direction and use\n\\[\nw(u)=\\left(u\\sqrt{1-|u|^2/4},\\,1-|u|^2/2\\right),\n\\qquad\ndw=(1-|u|^2/4)^{(n_*-2)/2}\\,du.\n\\]\nThe phase is exactly \\(1-|u|^2/2\\). The local integral is\n\\[\ne^{i\\Lambda}\\int e^{-i\\Lambda|u|^2/2}a_e(u)\\,du,\n\\]\nwith fixed support and \\(\\|a_e\\|_{C^m}\\lesssim_mA^m\\).</p><p>The damped Gaussian Fourier formula, followed by removal of damping, expresses this as \\(c_{n_*}\\Lambda^{-n_*/2}\\) times an integral involving\n\\(\\widehat a_e(\\xi)e^{i|\\xi|^2/(2\\Lambda)}\\). Taylor expansion gives remainder bounded by\n\\[\nC_J\\Lambda^{-J}\\int|\\xi|^{2J}|\\widehat a_e(\\xi)|\\,d\\xi\n \\le C_J\\Lambda^{-J}A^{2J+d+2}.\n\\]\nTake \\(J+\\lceil n_*/2\\rceil\\) integrations by parts for the nonstationary part to obtain the same extracted bound.</p><p>To identify the global coefficients, the full Fourier extension satisfies \\((\\Delta_\\xi+1)F=0\\). Its outgoing polar-cutoff part satisfies this equation modulo rapidly decreasing errors. In polar coordinates the operator is\n\\[\n\\partial_\\Lambda^2+\\frac{n_*}{\\Lambda}\\partial_\\Lambda+1\n +\\Lambda^{-2}\\Delta_{S^{n_*}}.\n\\]\nThe local expansion can be differentiated after taking enough terms. Substitution of\n\\(e^{i\\Lambda}\\Lambda^{-n_*/2}\\sum_j\\Lambda^{-j}a_j(e)\\) gives\n\\[\n2i(j+1)a_{j+1}\n =\\left(\\Delta_{S^{n_*}}+j(j+1)-\\frac{n_*(n_*-2)}4\\right)a_j.\n\\]\nStarting from \\(a_0=c_{n_*}b\\) yields \\(L_j\\). The minimum has the conjugate Gaussian phase.</p><p>For inversion, apply the direct expansion to \\(B_jb\\), through order \\(J-j-1\\). The defining recursion cancels all positive total indices below \\(J\\), while the zero coefficient is \\(b\\). The extracted remainder is at most\n\\[\nC_J\\Lambda^{-J}A^{2J+d+2}\n \\le C_JR^{-c\\sigma J+2(d+2)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(J\\) large for the required accuracy.</p><p>Radial coefficient derivatives cost fixed multiples of \\(\\Lambda^{-j}A^{2j}\\), hence remain bounded. More expansion terms handle any prescribed finite derivatives of remainders. Finally,\n\\[\n\\Delta_{S^{d-1}}=\\sum_{u&lt;v}\\Omega_{uv}^2.\n\\]\nLeibniz expansions differentiate but do not remove original mask factors; derivatives cannot enlarge their supports.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.6 — separated spatial expansion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On separated depth-\\(a\\) cells, for every fixed integer \\(j\\ge0\\), \\( (D(x,y)2^a)^{-j} =\\sum_\\ell c_\\ell f_\\ell(x)g_\\ell(y), \\) uniformly, where \\( |f_\\ell|,|g_\\ell|\\le1,\\qquad \\sum_\\ell|c_\\ell|\\le C_{d,j}. \\) The …", "statement": "<p>On separated depth-\\(a\\) cells, for every fixed integer \\(j\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n(D(x,y)2^a)^{-j}\n =\\sum_\\ell c_\\ell f_\\ell(x)g_\\ell(y),\n\\]\nuniformly, where\n\\[\n|f_\\ell|,|g_\\ell|\\le1,\\qquad\n\\sum_\\ell|c_\\ell|\\le C_{d,j}.\n\\]\nThe analogous assertion holds on the fixed outer separated supports, with geometry-dependent constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(x=c_X+2^{-a}u\\), \\(y=c_Y+2^{-a}z\\). Separation bounds\n\\[\n|2^a(c_X-c_Y)+u-z|\n\\]\nabove and below on a fixed neighborhood of the rescaled product boxes. Its negative \\(j\\)-th power has a smooth compactly supported extension with uniform derivative bounds. On a fixed \\(2d\\)-dimensional torus its Fourier coefficients decay as\n\\[\nC_m(1+|k|+|l|)^{-m}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(m&gt;2d\\). The series is absolutely summable, and each exponential separates into a function of \\(u\\) and one of \\(z\\), both of modulus one. Pull back to the original cells.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 8.1 and Corollary 8.4 — graph estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the regular class laws, symbols, transforms, and energies defined in Proposition \\(8\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the regular class laws, symbols, transforms, and energies defined in Proposition \\(8\\). Let \\(X,Y\\) be occupied depth-\\(a\\) cells, not necessarily separated, and let endpoint \\(j\\) have profile quantities \\(A_{j,n},I_j,k_j\\).</p><p>Assume \\(0\\le a&lt;p\\le v\\le N\\), and, with \\(L\\) the largest nominal length in either symbol,\n\\[\np-a\\le v-p+2\\sigma N,\\qquad\nL\\le v-p+2\\sigma N,\\qquad\nv-p\\ge10\\sigma N.\n\\]\nAt endpoint \\(j\\), assume the symbol imposes a parallel test \\([g_j,p]\\):\n\\[\nb_z^{(j)}(w)\\ne0\n \\Longrightarrow\n w\\in G^{(j)}_{\\ell_j,[g_j,p]}(z),\n\\qquad 0\\le\\ell_j\\le J_0,\n\\]\nfor almost every spatial point, where\n\\[\na\\le g_j&lt;p,\\qquad g_j\\le a+2\\sigma N.\n\\]</p><p>Then, for every fixed \\(T&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nH^{X,Y}(a,v)\n \\le\n R^{-\\sum_{j=1}^2(A_{j,p}-A_{j,a})\n       +\\min_jk_j(a,p)+O_d(\\sigma)+o(1)}\n \\max_{\\substack{P\\subset X,\\ Q\\subset Y\\\\\n                  P,Q\\ \\mathrm{occupied\\ depth}\\ p}}\n H^{P,Q}(p,v)\n +O(R^{-T}).\n\\]\nEvery child uses the same symbols restricted to its cells, retaining every factor, including factors anchored at \\(p\\).</p><p>In separated geometry the same bound holds with left side \\(H_F^{X,Y}(a,v)\\). This includes the outer root with separated actual supports inside possibly equal depth-zero cells.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact transform splitting is\n\\[\nU_X=R^{-I_1(a,p)}\\sum_{P\\subset X}U_P,\\qquad\nV_Y=R^{-I_2(a,p)}\\sum_{Q\\subset Y}V_Q.\n\\]\nApply Lemma \\(9.1\\). For retained row–column amplitudes \\(F_\\alpha,G_\\beta\\), use\n\\[\n\\sum_{(\\alpha,\\beta)\\ \\mathrm{edge}}|F_\\alpha G_\\beta|\n \\le\\frac{D_{\\mathrm{gr}}}{2}\n   \\left(\\sum_\\alpha|F_\\alpha|^2+\\sum_\\beta|G_\\beta|^2\\right).\n\\]\nOnly now are absolute values taken. Sum cap partition weights, whose sums are one, so no cap-count factor remains. Integrate the positive independent-angle child squares.</p><p>There are at most \\(R^{I_1+I_2+O_d(\\sigma)+o(1)}\\) row or column labels. The squared splitting factors contribute \\(R^{-2(I_1+I_2)}\\), and the ratio of energy prefactors is \\(2^{d(p-a)}\\). Since \\(S=d/2\\),\n\\[\nR^{-(I_1+I_2)}2^{d(p-a)}\n =R^{-\\sum_j(A_{j,p}-A_{j,a})}.\n\\]\nMultiply by Lemma \\(9.2\\)'s degree bound. Symbols were only restricted, so all factors remain. Finally, \\(H_F\\le H\\) with identical symbols proves original Corollary 8.4.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2 — nonstationary localization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition \\(9\\), put \\( h=p-a,\\quad q=v-p,\\quad \\delta=\\min(c_0,2^{-h}R^{10\\sigma}),\\quad \\tau=2^{-p}R^{30\\sigma}. \\) Take a nonnegative bounded-overlap spherical partition \\(\\{\\eta_\\nu\\}\\), supported in …", "statement": "<p>In Proposition \\(9\\), put\n\\[\nh=p-a,\\quad q=v-p,\\quad\n\\delta=\\min(c_0,2^{-h}R^{10\\sigma}),\\quad\n\\tau=2^{-p}R^{30\\sigma}.\n\\]\nTake a nonnegative bounded-overlap spherical partition \\(\\{\\eta_\\nu\\}\\), supported in radius-\\(C\\delta\\) caps centered at \\(w_\\nu\\), with\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla^m\\eta_\\nu\\|_\\infty\\le C_m\\delta^{-m}.\n\\]</p><p>Split endpoint transforms into depth-\\(p\\) pieces. For a cap pair, index a term by row \\((P,Q)\\) and column \\((P',Q')\\), and set\n\\[\nd_1=c_P-c_{P'},\\qquad d_2=c_Q-c_{Q'}.\n\\]\nA cell–cap incidence is eligible when\n\\[\n\\int_P\\int\\eta_\\nu(w)|b_x(w)|^2\\,dw\\,d\\rho(x)&gt;0.\n\\]\nRetain an edge when all incidences are eligible and\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{proj}_{(w_j^0)^\\perp}d_j|\\le\\tau\n\\quad(j=1,2),\\qquad\n|w_1^0\\cdot d_1+w_2^0\\cdot d_2|\\le\\tau.\n\\]</p><p>Replacing the expanded energy by the integrated sum over retained edges changes it by \\(O(R^{-T})\\), for every fixed \\(T&gt;0\\), before absolute values are taken.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed four spatial points put \\(\\Delta_1=x-x'\\), \\(\\Delta_2=y-y'\\). The phase is\n\\[\nr(w_1\\cdot\\Delta_1+w_2\\cdot\\Delta_2).\n\\]\nAll displacements join points in their own depth-\\(a\\) parent, so\n\\[\n|\\Delta_j|+|d_j|\\lesssim_d2^{-a}.\n\\]\nMoving centers to points and cap centers to cap angles changes each retained-edge test by at most\n\\[\nC_d(2^{-p}+2^{-a}\\delta)\n \\lesssim_d2^{-p}R^{10\\sigma}=o(\\tau).\n\\]</p><p>If the scalar test fails, throughout the whole term\n\\[\n|w_1\\cdot\\Delta_1+w_2\\cdot\\Delta_2|\\ge\\tau/2.\n\\]\nIntegration by parts in \\(u=r/2^v\\) gains\n\\[\n(2^v\\tau)^{-1},\\qquad\n2^v\\tau=2^qR^{30\\sigma}\\ge R^{40\\sigma}.\n\\]\nOnly the fixed radial cutoff is differentiated.</p><p>If a perpendicular test fails, choose the unit tangent at the corresponding cap center in the projected displacement direction and extend it by\n\\[\nV(w)=t-(t\\cdot w)w.\n\\]\nOn the support,\n\\[\n|V\\Phi|\\gtrsim2^v\\tau,\\qquad\n|V^m\\Phi|\\lesssim_m2^v2^{-a}.\n\\]\nAmplitude derivatives are bounded by\n\\(C_m(1+D_*+\\delta^{-1})^m\\). Repeated formal-adjoint integration by parts is bounded by a constant times\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1+D_*+\\delta^{-1}+2^{-a}/\\tau}{2^v\\tau}\\right)^m.\n\\]\nThe scale assumptions give\n\\[\nD_*\\le R^\\sigma+2^qR^{-47\\sigma},\\quad\n\\delta^{-1}\\le c_0^{-1}+2^qR^{-8\\sigma},\\quad\n2^{-a}/\\tau\\le2^qR^{-28\\sigma}.\n\\]\nThe ratio is at most \\(CR^{-26\\sigma}\\), giving arbitrary fixed-power decay.</p><p>Ineligible incidences contribute zero by Fubini. There are at most a fixed power of \\(R\\) cell quadruples, cap pairs, and normalization costs. Choose the integration order for a larger accuracy before summing. No spatial derivatives are taken, so arbitrary bounded measurable spatial multipliers are allowed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.3 — both graph degrees", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed cap pair in Lemma \\(9.1\\), both row and column degrees of the retained graph are bounded by \\( D_{\\mathrm{gr}} =R^{\\min_jk_j(a,p)+O_d(\\sigma)+o(1)}. \\) No separation between the two cap directions is …", "statement": "<p>For every fixed cap pair in Lemma \\(9.1\\), both row and column degrees of the retained graph are bounded by\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{gr}}\n =R^{\\min_jk_j(a,p)+O_d(\\sigma)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nNo separation between the two cap directions is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a row and consider one unknown of profile \\(j\\). Its perpendicular constraint places its center within \\(\\tau\\) of a fixed line in direction \\(w_j^0\\). Group candidates by depth-\\(g_j\\) ancestors; there are at most\n\\[\n2^{d(g_j-a)}\\le R^{2d\\sigma}\n\\]\ngroups.</p><p>In a nonempty group choose an eligible cell and a point–angle witness \\((z_*,w_*)\\) where the symbol is nonzero. Its parallel test is based at its anchor-cell center \\(c_*\\). Every other candidate center \\(c\\) in the group satisfies\n\\[\n|c-c_*|\\le\\sqrt d\\,2^{-g_j},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(c,c_*+\\mathbb Rw_*)\n \\lesssim_d2\\tau+2^{-g_j}\\delta\n \\lesssim_d2^{-p}R^{30\\sigma}.\n\\]\nThis is below all test widths \\(2^{-p}R^{500\\sigma}\\), and the testing ball contains the group. Thus the group has at most\n\\[\nR^{k_j(g_j,p)+C_d\\sigma}\n \\le R^{k_j(a,p)+C_d\\sigma}\n\\]\nmembers.</p><p>After this unknown is fixed, the scalar test confines the remaining unknown's longitudinal coordinate to an \\(O(\\tau)\\) interval in its own cap direction; its perpendicular test confines all other coordinates to radius \\(O(\\tau)\\). There are at most\n\\[\nC_d(1+2^p\\tau)^d\\lesssim_dR^{30d\\sigma}\n\\]\nremaining grid centers. Begin with either profile and take the better bound.</p><p>For a fixed column, reverse displacement signs and repeat. The scalar condition always controls the remaining coordinate in that unknown's own direction; no angle denominator occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 9.4 — single-profile state reductions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every nonterminal state with \\(i<J_0\\) is bounded, modulo \\(O(R^{-T})\\) oscillatory errors at any prescribed fixed accuracy, by polynomially many legal children and possibly a low-frequency stopping term.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one regular class \\(\\rho\\), with \\(M,A,I,k\\) as in Proposition \\(7\\). Both angular endpoint laws have this profile.</p><p class=\"li\">For separated depth-\\(a\\) cells \\(X,Y\\), define the positive pair restriction (original Definition 9.1)\n  \\[\n  \\theta_i^{X,Y}(a,t)=\n   \\mathbf1_{\\mathcal P_i^{X,Y}(a,t)}(\\rho|_X\\otimes\\rho|_Y),\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal P_i^{X,Y}(a,t)\\) consists of pairs satisfying every test in \\(\\mathcal M(a,t)\\) at level \\(i\\), at both endpoints, in direction \\(e(x,y)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(C_0=100(d+1)\\) and\n  \\[\n  D_i^{X,Y}(a,t)=2^{t-a}M(a)^{-4}\n   \\iint\\mathbf1_{\\{|D(x,y)-D(x',y')|\\le C_02^{-t}\\}}\n   \\,d\\theta_i^{X,Y}(a,t)(x,y)\n    d\\theta_i^{X,Y}(a,t)(x',y').\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A recursive angular state (original Definition 9.2) has retained base\n  \\(\\mathcal B=[a_0,t_0]\\), current interval \\([a,v]\\), and\n  \\[\n  a_0\\le a\\le v\\le t_0\\le N,\\qquad t_0\\le v+\\sigma N.\n  \\]\n  Its value \\(H_i^{\\mathcal B}(a,v)\\) is the independent energy of Proposition \\(8\\), on two occupied depth-\\(a\\) cells, with each symbol carrying exactly one occurrence of each descriptor in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M_{&gt;a}(\\mathcal B)\n   =\\{\\mathfrak t\\in\\mathcal M(a_0,t_0):\n          \\operatorname{anchor}(\\mathfrak t)&gt;a\\},\n  \\]\n  at common index \\(i&lt;J_0\\), fixed derivative budget, and fixed numerical angular bounds. No cell separation is required.</p><p class=\"li\">After a graph or near step, reached factors may be removed only from the resulting positive independent energy.</p><p class=\"li\">For angular states let\n  \\[\n  L_0=\\max(L,\\lceil10\\sigma N\\rceil),\\qquad h=v-2L_0,\n  \\]\n  where \\(L\\) is the largest remaining nominal length, or zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonterminal state with \\(i&lt;J_0\\) is bounded, modulo \\(O(R^{-T})\\) oscillatory errors at any prescribed fixed accuracy, by polynomially many legal children and possibly a low-frequency stopping term.</p><p>Except for low children, a child changing start \\(a\\) to \\(a'\\) has multiplier\n\\[\nR^{-2(A_{a'}-A_a)+\\mathrm{cost}+C_d\\sigma+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe transitions are:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{transition}&amp;\\text{parent and child}&amp;\\text{cost}\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{suffix}&amp;D_i(a,t)\\to D_{i+1}(a,p)&amp;\n A_p-\\min_{p\\le n\\le t}A_n\\\\\n\\text{low}&amp;D_i(a,t)\\to D_{i+1}(a,v)&amp;0\\\\\n\\text{high}&amp;D_i(a,t)\\to H_i^{[a,t]}(p,v)&amp;k(a,p)\\\\\n\\text{direct}&amp;H_i^{\\mathcal B}(a,v)\\to H_i^{\\mathcal B}(p,v)&amp;k(a,p)\\\\\n\\text{near}&amp;H_i^{\\mathcal B}(a,v)\\to H_i^{\\mathcal B}(h,v)&amp;0\\\\\n\\text{far}&amp;H_i^{\\mathcal B}(a,v)\\to D_{i+1}(b,v)&amp;0 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nA low child has full multiplier \\(R^{o(1)}\\), without fixed \\(C_d\\sigma\\) loss. A low-frequency stopping term is \\(R^{C_d\\sigma+o(1)}\\).</p><p>For positive states, \\(p\\) is the fixed policy choice. Suffix is used when \\(2p&gt;a+t\\); otherwise \\(a&lt;v\\le t\\), with low shells satisfying \\(t-v\\ge\\sigma N\\) and high shells \\(t-v&lt;\\sigma N\\).</p><p>If \\(h\\le a\\), a direct step uses a maximal-length remaining descriptor, with anchor \\(p\\) and an origin \\([b_0,u_0]\\subset\\mathcal B\\); then \\(L=L_0\\), \\(a&lt;p&lt;v\\),\n\\[\nb_0\\le p-L,\\qquad p+L\\le u_0,\\qquad v-a\\le2L,\n\\]\nand the supplied parallel test is \\([\\max(a,p-L),p]\\).</p><p>If \\(h&gt;a\\), near uses depth \\(h\\); far uses separated depth-\\(b\\) pairs with\n\\[\na&lt;b\\le h+O_d(1)&lt;v.\n\\]\nDirect and near retain the base, index, and fixed symbol bounds; far has fresh positive list \\(\\mathcal M(b,v)\\).</p><p>Every terminal state is bounded by\n\\[\nR^{C_d(\\kappa+\\sigma)+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>**Suffix.** Let \\(\\theta=\\theta_i^{X,Y}(a,t)\\). Retain depth-\\(p\\) cell pairs intersecting its support and choose one witness in each. With \\(e_0=e(c_P,c_Q)\\), parent separation and the Hessian estimate give\n\\[\nD(x,y)=e_0\\cdot(x-y)+O_d(2^{a-2p})\n      =e_0\\cdot(x-y)+O_d(2^{-t}).\n\\]\nThe witness angle differs from \\(e_0\\) by \\(O_d(2^{a-p})\\), changing source projection differences by \\(O_d(2^{a-2p})\\). The full conditional projection test \\([p,t]\\) therefore bounds conditional self-collision by\n\\[\nB=2^{-(t-p)}\n R^{A_p-\\min_{p\\le n\\le t}A_n+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nLemma \\(10.1\\) gives the same bound for translated windows. Fixing the other endpoint variables yields\n\\[\nB_{2^{-t}}\\!\\left(D_\\#(\\rho|_P\\otimes\\rho|_Q)\\right)\n \\lesssim B\\rho(P)^2\\rho(Q)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Group retained cell pairs by their center-distance bin of width \\(2^{-p}\\), and let \\(W_G\\) be the sum of their product masses. The \\(\\ell^2\\) triangle inequality and bounded overlap of enlarged coarse bins bound fine collision by \\(CB\\sum_GW_G^2\\).</p><p>Every full pair in a retained \\(P\\times Q\\) satisfies \\(\\mathcal M(a,p)\\) at level \\(i+1\\): anchors agree with the witness, lengths are at most \\((p-a)/2\\), and direction changes lie inside Lemma \\(7.3\\)'s buffer. Pairs in one group have distance difference below \\(C_02^{-p}\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_GW_G^2\\le2^{-(p-a)}M(a)^4D_{i+1}(a,p).\n\\]\nAfter parent normalization, the factor is\n\\[\n2^{t-a}M(a)^{-4}B\\,2^{-(p-a)}M(a)^4\n =R^{A_p-\\min_{[p,t]}A+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]</p><p>**Prefix conversion and low shells.** Define the positive distance measure\n\\[\n\\zeta=D_\\#\\!\\left[\n M(a)^{-2}(D2^a)^{-(d-1)/2}\n \\prod_{\\mathfrak t\\in\\mathcal M(a,t)}\n w_{i,\\mathfrak t}(x,e)w_{i,\\mathfrak t}(y,e)\n (\\rho|_X\\otimes\\rho|_Y)\\right].\n\\]\nMasks equal one on \\(\\theta\\), and the distance weight is bounded above and below. Positivity and Lemma \\(10.1\\) imply\n\\[\nD_i(a,t)\\lesssim2^{-a}\\int_{|r|\\le2^t}|\\widehat\\zeta(r)|^2\\,dr.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\zeta(\\mathbb R)\\le R^{C_d\\sigma}\\), frequencies below \\(2^a\\) give the stopping term. Split positive higher frequencies into dyadic shells \\(a&lt;v\\le t\\).</p><p>For \\(t-v\\ge\\sigma N\\), a smooth shell majorant has inverse transform bounded by\n\\[\nC2^v(1+2^v|s-s'|)^{-2}.\n\\]\nOn the support of old masks, all tests in \\(\\mathcal M(a,v)\\) hold at level \\(i+1\\). Lemma \\(10.1\\) bounds this contribution by \\(CD_{i+1}(a,v)\\). The exact model mass is the same on both sides, so no fixed mass-comparison loss occurs. Shell summation is subpower.</p><p>**High shells.** If \\(t-v&lt;\\sigma N\\), list geometry and nonterminality give\n\\[\nv-a&gt;20\\sigma N,\\qquad L\\le(v-a)/2+\\sigma N.\n\\]\nApply Proposition \\(8\\). For a prefix choice, \\([a,p]\\) is present and\n\\[\np-a\\le v-p+\\sigma N,\\quad\nL\\le v-p+\\sigma N,\\quad\nv-p&gt;10\\sigma N.\n\\]\nProposition \\(9\\) applies with \\(g=a\\). In its positive independent children, remove reached factors. The remaining list is exactly \\(\\mathcal M_{&gt;p}([a,t])\\), and\n\\[\na\\le p\\le v\\le t\\le v+\\sigma N.\n\\]\nThis gives the high multiplier\n\\[\nR^{-2(A_p-A_a)+k(a,p)+C_d\\sigma+o(1)}.\n\\]\nConverted normalized mask derivatives retain their level-\\((i+1)\\) supports, so the angular index remains \\(i\\).</p><p>**Direct.** If \\(h\\le a\\), nonterminality forces \\(L=L_0\\). A longest descriptor's origin gives\n\\[\nb_0\\le p-L,\\quad p+L\\le u_0\\le v+\\sigma N,\\quad v-a\\le2L.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nL\\le v-p+\\sigma N,\\quad\np-a\\le v-p+2\\sigma N,\\quad\nv-p&gt;10\\sigma N.\n\\]\nFor \\(g=\\max(a,p-L)\\),\n\\[\na\\le g&lt;p,\\qquad g\\le a+\\sigma N.\n\\]\nThe origin supplies \\([g,p]\\). Proposition \\(9\\) gives cost \\(k(a,p)\\). Remove reached factors only in positive child energies, leaving \\(\\mathcal M_{&gt;p}(\\mathcal B)\\).</p><p>**Near.** If \\(h&gt;a\\), split transforms at depth \\(h\\), retaining all factors. A term is near when both within-parent center displacements are at most \\(10(d+1)2^{-h}\\). Its row and column degrees are bounded. Apply \\(2|uv|\\le|u|^2+|v|^2\\). There are at most \\(R^{2I(a,h)+C_d\\sigma}\\) labels; splitting and energy normalizations give\n\\[\n2^{d(h-a)}R^{-4I(a,h)}R^{2I(a,h)+C_d\\sigma}\n =R^{-2(A_h-A_a)+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nRemove reached factors after forming positive child energies. The remaining list is \\(\\mathcal M_{&gt;h}(\\mathcal B)\\).</p><p>**Far: oscillation.** In remaining terms at least one point displacement has length \\(\\gtrsim_d2^{-h}\\). Write lengths \\(d_+\\ge d_-\\). For the larger displacement,\n\\[\n\\Lambda=rd_+\\gtrsim2^{v-h}=2^{2L_0},\n\\]\nand Lemma \\(8.2\\)'s small-derivative-scale hypotheses hold, with\n\\(D_*^2/\\Lambda\\lesssim R^{-18\\sigma}\\) or \\(R^{-98\\sigma}\\).\nExpand its full-sphere integral. Extracted stationary coefficients and fixed \\(r/2^v\\) derivatives are bounded.</p><p>If \\(d_+&gt;2d_-\\), leave the other angular integral exact. Each radial phase derivative has modulus at least \\(d_+/2\\), so repeated radial integration by parts gives arbitrary-power error.</p><p>Otherwise expand both angular integrals. Choose \\(n\\) with\n\\(d_+\\in(2^{-n-1},2^{-n}]\\), and put \\(b=n+\\log_2K_d\\). Both internal pairs lie in separated depth-\\(b\\) geometry after moving to centers, and\n\\[\na&lt;b\\le h+O_d(1)&lt;v.\n\\]\nThe two stationary gains and radial integration bound every sign contribution by\n\\[\nC2^{-(d-1)(v-b)}\n (1+2^v|d_1-d_2|)^{-2}.\n\\]</p><p>**Far: support and normalization.** Since \\([b,v]\\subset\\mathcal B\\), its positive list is a sublist of the retained base list. All its anchors exceed \\(b&gt;a\\), so its factors were present in the far symbols. Angular differentiation retains their supports. Evaluation at either stationary direction imposes level-\\((i+1)\\) tests at both endpoints of each internal pair; antipodal symmetry handles negative directions.</p><p>Opening the four depth-\\(h\\) transforms cancels their splitting denominators:\n\\[\n(M(h)/M(a))^4M(h)^{-4}=M(a)^{-4}.\n\\]\nAfter the nonnegative kernel bound, discard the far-label selector while retaining the comparable length band. Summing the disjoint \\(h\\)-cell partition reassembles the raw four-point measure, then repartition at depth \\(b\\). No \\(h\\)-label count remains.</p><p>For a fixed separated depth-\\(b\\) quadruple, let \\(\\eta_1,\\eta_2\\) be the two positive distance measures with child tests. Lemma \\(10.1\\) bounds the kernel integral by\n\\[\nM(b)^4\\,2^{-(v-b)}\\max D_{i+1}(b,v).\n\\]\nEach first internal cell has only dimensionally many possible partners at distance \\(O_d(K_d2^{-b})\\), so there are at most \\(R^{2I(a,b)+C_d\\sigma}\\) quadruples. The full factor is\n\\[\n2^{d(v-a)}M(a)^{-4}\n 2^{-(d-1)(v-b)}M(b)^4\\,2^{-(v-b)}\n R^{2I(a,b)+C_d\\sigma}\n =R^{-2(A_b-A_a)+C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nScales and fixed coefficient expansions cost subpower factors.</p><p>**Stopping.** If width is at most \\(100q_0\\), the definitions, bounded symbols, and cell-mass comparisons give\n\\[\nH_i^{\\mathcal B}(a,v)\\lesssim2^{d(v-a)}R^{C_d\\sigma},\n\\qquad\nD_i(a,t)\\lesssim2^{t-a}R^{C_d\\sigma}.\n\\]\nThese are \\(R^{C_d(\\kappa+\\sigma)+o(1)}\\). All constructions have polynomially many discrete children and the stipulated symbol bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.3 — binning positive measures", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For finite positive line measures \\(\\eta,\\zeta\\), \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), define \\( B_\\varepsilon(\\eta) =\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\\eta([j\\varepsilon,(j+1)\\varepsilon))^2. \\) For every fixed \\(C\\ge1\\) …", "statement": "<p>For finite positive line measures \\(\\eta,\\zeta\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon(\\eta)\n =\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\\eta([j\\varepsilon,(j+1)\\varepsilon))^2.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(C\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon(\\eta)\n \\le(\\eta\\otimes\\eta)\\{|s-s'|\\le\\varepsilon\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_u(\\eta\\otimes\\eta)\\{|s-s'-u|\\le C\\varepsilon\\}\n \\le C'B_\\varepsilon(\\eta),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\iint(1+|s-s'|/\\varepsilon)^{-2}\\,d\\eta(s)d\\zeta(s')\n \\le C'B_\\varepsilon(\\eta)^{1/2}B_\\varepsilon(\\zeta)^{1/2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(\\eta\\otimes\\eta)\\{|s-s'|\\le C\\varepsilon\\}\n \\le C'\\varepsilon\\int_{|r|\\le\\varepsilon^{-1}}\n        |\\widehat\\eta(r)|^2\\,dr.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(b_j,c_j\\) for bin masses. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_jb_jb_{j+h}\\le\\sum_jb_j^2,\\qquad\n\\sum_jb_jc_{j+h}\\le\n \\left(\\sum_jb_j^2\\sum_jc_j^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nA fixed-width translated window permits boundedly many bin offsets. For the decaying kernel, sum these bounds against \\(C(1+|h|)^{-2}\\).</p><p>For the Fourier estimate choose sufficiently small fixed \\(b&gt;0\\) and\n\\[\nK(t)=(\\sin(bt)/(bt))^2.\n\\]\nIt is bounded below on \\([-C,C]\\), and its nonnegative Fourier transform is bounded and supported in \\([-1,1]\\). Apply Fourier inversion and Fubini to \\(K((s-s')/\\varepsilon)\\). No density hypothesis is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem 10.4 — single-profile angular bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniformly over its occupied cells, including two copies of the same cell, and over its allowed symbols and bounded spatial multipliers …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the single-profile recursive states of Proposition \\(10\\), with \\(q_0=\\lceil\\kappa N\\rceil\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer interval \\(J=[a,t]\\) of width at least \\(10q_0\\), define the two-depth potential (original Definition 10.1)\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V(J)=\n  \\min_{\\substack{a\\le i&lt;j\\le t\\\\j-i\\ge10q_0}}(A_i+A_j).\n  \\]\n  Leave it undefined for shorter intervals.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_0^{[c,N]}(c,N)\\) be a nonterminal recursive angular state. Fix its initial derivative budget and common numerical angular bounds independently of \\(N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over its occupied cells, including two copies of the same cell, and over its allowed symbols and bounded spatial multipliers,\n\\[\nH_0^{[c,N]}(c,N)\n \\le R^{2A_c-\\mathcal V([c,N])\n              +C_d(\\kappa+\\sigma/\\kappa)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe \\(o(1)\\) may depend on the fixed derivative budget and angular bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Iterate Proposition \\(10\\), stopping at terminal states and low-frequency stops. Lemma \\(11.2\\) bounds branch depth by a constant depending only on fixed parameters and guarantees the smoothing budget. Polynomial branching and subpower coefficient sums cost only \\(R^{o(1)}\\). Suprema over allowed symbols require no counting.</p><p>For one branch, let \\(a_f\\) be the final start. Start increments telescope to\n\\[\n2A_c-2A_{a_f}.\n\\]\nBy Lemma \\(11.1\\), costs telescope through defined potentials. If a child has width below \\(10q_0\\), the step reaching it has zero cost. Let \\(J_*=[u,w]\\) be the last interval with defined potential. Then\n\\[\n\\sum\\mathrm{cost}\n \\le\\mathcal V(J_*)-\\mathcal V([c,N]).\n\\]\nThis also covers a low-frequency stop at a nonterminal interval.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\ni=\\max\\{u,\\min(a_f,w-10q_0)\\},\\qquad j=i+10q_0.\n\\]\nBoth depths lie in \\(J_*\\), are admissibly separated, and are within \\(10q_0\\) of \\(a_f\\). The Lipschitz profile bound gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(J_*)\\le A_i+A_j\n \\le2A_{a_f}+20dq_0/N.\n\\]\nAfter cancellation, the exponent is\n\\[\n2A_c-\\mathcal V([c,N])+O_d(\\kappa)+o(1).\n\\]\nStopping costs \\(O_d(\\kappa+\\sigma)\\). Only non-low transitions incur fixed \\(C_d\\sigma\\) loss, giving total \\(O_d(\\sigma/\\kappa)\\).</p><p>Fix all accuracy and derivative budgets before taking \\(N\\) large. Differences of \\(A\\) and costs are dimensionally bounded, so each ancestor factor is at most \\(R^{C_d+o(1)}\\). Choose local accuracy\n\\[\nT&gt;C_d(1/\\kappa+1/\\sigma)+10d+10\n\\]\nwith sufficient constant to absorb all propagation.</p><p>There is no recursive increase in persistent derivative budget. Every positive-to-angular conversion starts with fresh undifferentiated masks; choose its inverse expansion order for this fixed accuracy. Graph and near steps retain or remove factors but add no derivatives. Far expansions may require higher direct orders, but their children are positive states and retain none of those derivatives. Choose direct and nonstationary orders after the persistent budget is fixed. All are independent of \\(N\\), and their constants enter only permitted constants and \\(o(1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.2 — payment of costs", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every potentially positive-cost reduction in Proposition \\(10\\), with parent interval \\(J\\), child interval \\(J'\\), and stated cost …", "statement": "<p>For every potentially positive-cost reduction in Proposition \\(10\\), with parent interval \\(J\\), child interval \\(J'\\), and stated cost,\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(J')\\text{ is defined},\\qquad\n\\mathrm{cost}\\le\\mathcal V(J')-\\mathcal V(J).\n\\]\nThe same inequality holds for every zero-cost reduction whose child potential is defined.</p>", "proof": "<p>Containment of admissible pairs proves all zero-cost cases.</p><p>For suffix, \\(J=[a,t]\\), \\(J'=[a,p]\\), and child width exceeds half the nonterminal parent width, hence exceeds \\(10q_0\\). For an admissible child pair \\((i,j)\\), \\(j\\ge a+10q_0\\) and \\(j\\le p\\), so \\(j\\) lies in the policy's whole-block inner interval and \\(A_j\\ge A_p\\). Choose \\(r\\in[p,t]\\) minimizing \\(A\\). The parent pair \\((i,r)\\) remains admissible and\n\\[\nA_i+A_r\\le A_i+A_j-\\bigl(A_p-\\min_{[p,t]}A\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>For high prefix, \\(J'=[p,v]\\) has width\n\\[\nv-p\\ge(t-a)/2-\\sigma N&gt;10q_0.\n\\]\nFor admissible \\((i,j)\\), one has\n\\[\np\\le i\\le v-10q_0\\le t-10q_0,\n\\]\nso \\(i\\) is in the origin's inner interval and \\(A_i\\ge A_p\\). Replace \\(i\\) by a minimizer \\(r\\in[a,p]\\); separation is preserved and the sum falls by at least \\(k(a,p)\\).</p><p>For direct, let \\([b_0,u_0]\\) be the chosen longest descriptor's origin, so\n\\[\nb_0\\le p-L,\\qquad p+L\\le u_0,\\qquad v\\le a+2L.\n\\]\nChild width again exceeds \\(10q_0\\). If \\(k(a,p)=0\\), containment suffices. Otherwise choose\n\\[\ni_0\\in[a,p],\\qquad A_{i_0}=A_p-k(a,p)&lt;A_p.\n\\]\nSince the origin's policy minimizes its inner interval, \\(i_0\\) lies strictly to its left:\n\\[\na\\le i_0&lt;b_0+2q_0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nv&lt;b_0+2q_0+2L\\le u_0+2q_0.\n\\]\nFor any admissible child pair,\n\\[\np\\le i\\le v-10q_0&lt;u_0-8q_0.\n\\]\nThus \\(i\\) lies in the origin's inner interval and \\(A_i\\ge A_p\\). Replace it by \\(i_0\\). The new pair lies in the actual parent \\([a,v]\\), remains separated, and decreases the sum by at least \\(k(a,p)\\). Apply these comparisons to minimizing child pairs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 10.3 — finite policy and smoothing budget", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently small fixed parameters and sufficiently large \\(N\\), every branch of Proposition \\(10\\)'s reductions has at most \\(20/\\kappa\\) non-low transitions and at most \\(1/\\sigma\\) low transitions.", "statement": "<p>For sufficiently small fixed parameters and sufficiently large \\(N\\), every branch of Proposition \\(10\\)'s reductions has at most \\(20/\\kappa\\) non-low transitions and at most \\(1/\\sigma\\) low transitions.</p><p>The smoothing index increases by at most \\(20/\\kappa+1/\\sigma\\). Starting at zero, every required mask index and next support level is strictly below\n\\[\nJ_0=\\left\\lceil100(1/\\kappa+1/\\sigma)+100\\right\\rceil.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Starts never decrease and ends never increase. Let \\(K\\le1/\\kappa+2\\) be the number of fixed block representatives. Every high or direct step advances the start to a representative; there are at most \\(K\\) such steps. Every suffix step lowers the end by at least \\(q_0\\), so there are at most \\(1/\\kappa\\). Every low step lowers it by at least \\(\\sigma N\\), so there are at most \\(1/\\sigma\\).</p><p>An angular episode begins at the root or after a high step and ends at a far step or stop. There are at most \\(K+1\\) episodes and far steps.</p><p>For a near step with nonterminal child, the floor in \\(L_0\\) is inactive. If removing reached factors decreases the maximal remaining length, charge the step to a maximal anchor just passed; charges are distinct. If maximal length stays \\(L\\), the new start is \\(v-2L\\), so the next step is direct; charge to it. There is at most one near step with terminal child. Hence at most \\(2K+1\\) near steps occur.</p><p>The combined non-low count is at most \\(5K+2&lt;20/\\kappa\\). Only suffix, low, and far increase the index, each by one. The stated \\(J_0\\) also accommodates the next level-\\((i+1)\\) support.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 11.3, with Lemmas 11.1–11.2 — positive approximants and shell decay", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The parameters can be chosen in the order \\(\\beta,\\kappa,\\sigma,\\omega\\) so that, for some \\(\\eta,\\gamma>0\\), \\( 0\\le\\tau_N\\le\\alpha_N,\\qquad \\|\\alpha_N-\\tau_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\lesssim2^{-\\eta N}, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the nonclassical alternative of Proposition \\(5\\), with fixed separated original probabilities \\(\\mu_1,\\mu_2\\), exponent \\(S+\\chi\\), and decreasing filters \\(\\Gamma_N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">By one common similarity place \\(E\\) inside \\((1/4,3/4)^d\\); choose a common translated dyadic grid with zero boundary mass and root cube \\(Q_0\\) containing \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Regularize each \\(\\mu_j\\) with parameter \\(\\sigma\\). Denote retained classes by \\(\\mathcal C_{j,N}\\), their original masses by\n  \\[\n  a_{j,C}=\\mu_j(C),\n  \\qquad \\rho_{j,C}=a_{j,C}^{-1}\\mu_j|_C.\n  \\]\n  Their count is \\(R^{o(1)}\\), and discarded original mass is \\(R^{-\\sigma+o(1)}\\). Every root model mass is exactly one.</p><p class=\"li\">For a class pair write \\(A_{j,n}=m_j(n/N)-Sn/N\\). Define its entry depth\n  \\[\n  c=\\max\\{0\\le n\\le N:\\min(A_{1,n},A_{2,n})\\le\\beta\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At endpoint \\(j\\), use the extra parallel descriptor \\([0,c]\\) and the list \\(\\mathcal M_j(c,N)\\), and set\n  \\[\n  b_{j;C_1,C_2}(z,w)=\n   w^{(j)}_{0,[0,c]}(z,w)\n   \\prod_{\\mathfrak t\\in\\mathcal M_j(c,N)}\n      w^{(j)}_{0,\\mathfrak t}(z,w).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D(x,y)=|x-y|\\), \\(e(x,y)=(x-y)/D(x,y)\\), and\n  \\[\n  W(x,y)=D(x,y)^{-(d-1)/2}.\n  \\]\n  Original cross distances are between fixed positive constants, so \\(W\\) is bounded above and below.</p><p class=\"li\">On a retained class pair define\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_N(x,y)=\n   b_{1;C_1,C_2}(x,e(x,y))\n   b_{2;C_1,C_2}(y,e(x,y)),\n  \\]\n  and set \\(\\Theta_N=0\\) when either endpoint is outside retained classes.</p><p class=\"li\">Define positive scalar measures\n  \\[\n  \\alpha_N=D_\\#\\!\\left[W\\mathbf1_{\\Gamma_N}(\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)\\right],\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\tau_N=D_\\#\\!\\left[W\\mathbf1_{\\Gamma_N}\\Theta_N\n                      (\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)\\right].\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The parameters can be chosen in the order \\(\\beta,\\kappa,\\sigma,\\omega\\) so that, for some \\(\\eta,\\gamma&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\\tau_N\\le\\alpha_N,\\qquad\n\\|\\alpha_N-\\tau_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\lesssim2^{-\\eta N},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{2^{N-1}\\le|r|\\le2^{N+1}}\n |\\widehat{\\tau_N}(r)|^2\\,dr\\lesssim2^{-\\gamma N}.\n\\]\nMoreover \\(\\alpha_N\\) decreases to the nonzero positive measure\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\infty=\nD_\\#\\!\\left[W\\mathbf1_{\\Gamma_\\infty}(\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)\\right],\n\\qquad\n\\Gamma_\\infty=\\bigcap_N\\Gamma_N.\n\\]\nAll these measures are carried by the compact original distance set \\(\\Delta(E)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(12.2\\) gives approximation, positivity, monotonicity, and support. It remains to prove shell decay.</p><p>Fix one normalized class pair and one spatial product \\(X_\\ell\\times Y_\\ell\\) in the partition of \\(\\Gamma_N\\). Let \\(\\nu\\) be its weighted masked distance component, retaining its spatial indicators as bounded measurable endpoint multipliers. At the root use \\(X=Y=Q_0\\), \\(a=0\\), \\(v=N\\); the actual supported laws are separated, and both model masses are one.</p><p>Lemma \\(7.5\\) and \\(c&lt;N/4\\) give\n\\[\nL\\le\\max(c,(N-c)/2)\\le N/2.\n\\]\nProposition \\(8\\) therefore bounds the positive shell norm by\n\\[\nR^{o(1)}\\sup H_F(0,N)+O(R^{-T}).\n\\]\nIts root weight is precisely \\(W\\), and its symbols retain both lists and index zero.</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(9\\) at \\(p=c\\), using the extra \\([0,c]\\) at both endpoints. The scale conditions follow from \\(c&lt;N/4\\), \\(L\\le N/2\\), and \\(N-c\\ge10\\sigma N\\). Its factor is\n\\[\nR^{-A_{1,c}-A_{2,c}\n    +\\min_jk_j(0,c)+O_d(\\sigma)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nIn each positive independent child, remove the reached extra factor, including any normalized derivatives produced by conversion. Remaining lists are exactly the unreached descriptors of the bases \\([c,N]\\).</p><p>Write endpoint angular energies as nonnegative \\(E_1(r),E_2(r)\\), with common measure \\(d\\lambda_N=\\Psi(r/2^N)\\,dr/2^N\\). Radial Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n2^{d(N-c)}\\int E_1E_2\\,d\\lambda_N\n \\le\n \\left(2^{d(N-c)}\\int E_1^2\\,d\\lambda_N\\right)^{1/2}\n \\left(2^{d(N-c)}\\int E_2^2\\,d\\lambda_N\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nEach factor is a single-profile recursive energy obtained by duplicating one endpoint law, cell, and symbol. No separation is required. Proposition \\(11\\) bounds it by\n\\[\nR^{2A_{j,c}-\\mathcal V_j([c,N])\n       +O_d(\\kappa+\\sigma/\\kappa)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThus all profile increments cancel, and Lemma \\(12.1\\) gives the remaining principal exponent\n\\[\n\\min_jk_j(0,c)\n -\\frac12\\bigl(\\mathcal V_1([c,N])+\\mathcal V_2([c,N])\\bigr)\n \\le-\\beta+C_d\\sigma+2d/N.\n\\]\nUniformly for every component,\n\\[\n\\int_{2^{N-1}\\le r\\le2^{N+1}}|\\widehat\\nu(r)|^2\\,dr\n \\lesssim R^{-\\beta+C_d(\\kappa+\\sigma/\\kappa)+o(1)}+R^{-T}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose parameters in order. Fix \\(\\beta\\), then \\(\\kappa\\) so entry is nonterminal and \\(C_d\\kappa&lt;\\beta/16\\); choose \\(\\sigma\\) so all hypotheses hold and \\(C_d\\sigma/\\kappa&lt;\\beta/16\\); finally choose\n\\[\n4d\\omega&lt;\\beta/8.\n\\]\nPrepare \\(\\Gamma_N\\) with this fixed \\(\\omega\\) and angular loss \\(R^\\sigma\\). Its exceptional deletion exponent need only be positive.</p><p>Fix local oscillatory accuracy after the parameters and before \\(N\\), sufficiently large to absorb all ancestor factors and leave component remainder \\(R^{-T}\\), say \\(T&gt;10+4d\\omega\\). Fix inverse orders and persistent symbol budgets first; then direct and nonstationary orders. These choices affect constants and thresholds, not the dimensional exponent losses.</p><p>There are \\(L_N\\lesssim R^{2d\\omega}\\) spatial products, and exact recombination is\n\\[\n\\tau_N=\\sum_{C_1,C_2}a_{1,C_1}a_{2,C_2}\n                 \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{L_N}\\nu_{C_1,C_2,\\ell}.\n\\]\nThe Fourier \\(L^2\\) triangle inequality costs no class-count factor because\n\\[\n\\sum_{C_1,C_2}a_{1,C_1}a_{2,C_2}\\le1.\n\\]\nSpatial products cost \\(L_N\\) before squaring. Hence\n\\[\n\\int_{2^{N-1}\\le r\\le2^{N+1}}|\\widehat{\\tau_N}(r)|^2\\,dr\n \\lesssim\n R^{-\\beta+C_d(\\kappa+\\sigma/\\kappa)+4d\\omega+o(1)}\n +R^{-T+4d\\omega}.\n\\]\nThe chosen parameters leave a negative exponent, for example \\(-\\beta/2\\) for sufficiently large \\(N\\). Since \\(\\tau_N\\) is positive and real, negative frequencies are conjugates. This proves the two-sided shell estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 11.1 — entry depth", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<\\beta<\\min(\\chi/10,1/10)\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\beta&lt;\\min(\\chi/10,1/10)\\). For sufficiently small \\(\\sigma\\) and large \\(N\\), the entry depth in Proposition \\(12\\) is defined and satisfies\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\beta N}{2d}\\right\\rfloor\\le c&lt;N/4,\n\\]\n\\[\nA_{j,n}\\ge\\beta-d/N\n \\qquad(j=1,2,\\ c\\le n\\le N),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\min_{j=1,2}k_j(0,c)\\le\\beta+C_d\\sigma.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(\\kappa\\), \\([c,N]\\) is nonterminal and\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_j([c,N])\n =\\min_{\\substack{c\\le u&lt;v\\le N\\\\v-u\\ge10q_0}}\n       (A_{j,u}+A_{j,v})\n \\ge2\\beta-2d/N.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The root index qualifies. For \\(n\\le\\beta N/(2d)\\), Lipschitz continuity and \\(A_{j,0}=0\\) give \\(A_{j,n}\\le\\beta/2\\). For \\(n\\ge N/4\\), the profile lower bound gives\n\\[\nA_{j,n}\\ge\\chi/4-C_d\\sigma&gt;\\beta.\n\\]\nThis proves both bounds on \\(c\\).</p><p>By maximality, both \\(A_{j,c+1}&gt;\\beta\\), hence \\(A_{j,c}&gt;\\beta-d/N\\); later values exceed \\(\\beta\\). At least one side \\(j_*\\) has \\(A_{j_*,c}\\le\\beta\\), and\n\\(\\min_{0\\le n\\le c}A_{j_*,n}\\ge-C_d\\sigma\\), proving the entry-cost bound.</p><p>The interval width exceeds \\(3N/4\\), so it is nonterminal for small \\(\\kappa\\). Both depths in every admissible potential pair satisfy the stated lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma 11.2 — restoration of mass", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the measures in Proposition \\(12\\), use Proposition \\(5\\)'s cap preparation with angular loss \\(R^\\sigma\\).", "statement": "<p>For the measures in Proposition \\(12\\), use Proposition \\(5\\)'s cap preparation with angular loss \\(R^\\sigma\\). Then, for some \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\\tau_N\\le\\alpha_N,\\qquad\n\\|\\alpha_N-\\tau_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\lesssim2^{-\\eta N}.\n\\]\nThe measures \\(\\alpha_N\\) decrease to the nonzero \\(\\alpha_\\infty\\) specified there, and all measures are carried by \\(\\Delta(E)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Cap preparation gives \\(G_N\\subset\\Gamma_N\\) with\n\\[\n(\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)(\\Gamma_N\\setminus G_N)\\lesssim R^{-c_*}\n\\]\nfor some \\(c_*&gt;0\\). At almost every first endpoint,\n\\[\n\\xi_{N,x}=e(x,\\cdot)_\\#\n  [\\mathbf1_{G_N}(x,\\cdot)\\mu_2]\n\\]\nis an unnormalized spherical subprobability with \\(S\\)-cap loss \\(R^\\sigma\\); similarly in reverse. Antipodal change preserves the cap bound.</p><p>Fix a class pair and one first-endpoint descriptor. At \\(x\\) in its retained class, the anchor is occupied. Lemma \\(7.2\\) applies directly to \\(\\xi_{N,x}\\), so its failing directions capture opposite original mass at most \\(R^{-\\sigma}\\), also after restricting that opposite endpoint to the specified class. Integrating over the first class costs at most \\(a_{1,C_1}R^{-\\sigma}\\). Sum first over first-side classes using their original weights, then over second-side classes and descriptors, whose counts are \\(R^{o(1)}\\). Failure mass is \\(R^{-\\sigma+o(1)}\\). Repeat in reverse orientation. There is no sum over anchor cells: a point and descriptor already determine one.</p><p>All masks equal one when all level-zero tests hold. Including discarded classes and \\(\\Gamma_N\\setminus G_N\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Gamma_N}(1-\\Theta_N)\\,d(\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2)\n \\lesssim R^{-c_*}+R^{-\\sigma+o(1)}.\n\\]\nBoundedness of \\(W\\) proves the total-variation estimate, for example with\n\\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\min(c_*,\\sigma)/2\\).</p><p>The cap-controlled \\(G_N\\) was used only for this estimate, not as a multiplier defining \\(\\tau_N\\). Monotonicity of \\(\\Gamma_N\\) and continuity of finite measures on decreasing sets prove the limit formula. Positive limiting product mass and the positive lower bound of \\(W\\) give nonzero limit. Continuity of \\(D\\) and compact original supports show all pushforwards are carried by \\(\\Delta(E)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Lemma 11.4 — monotone measure shell criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\((\\alpha_N)_{N\\ge K}\\) be finite positive Borel measures on \\(\\mathbb R\\) with \\( \\alpha_{N+1}\\le\\alpha_N,\\qquad \\alpha_\\infty=\\lim_N\\alpha_N. \\) Let \\(\\tau_N\\) be finite complex Borel measures such that …", "statement": "<p>Let \\((\\alpha_N)_{N\\ge K}\\) be finite positive Borel measures on \\(\\mathbb R\\) with\n\\[\n\\alpha_{N+1}\\le\\alpha_N,\\qquad \\alpha_\\infty=\\lim_N\\alpha_N.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\tau_N\\) be finite complex Borel measures such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{N\\ge K}\\|\\alpha_N-\\tau_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{N\\ge K}\n \\left(\\int_{2^{N-1}\\le|r|\\le2^{N+1}}\n          |\\widehat{\\tau_N}(r)|^2\\,dr\\right)^{1/2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\infty\\ll\\mathcal L^1.\n\\]\nIn particular, the hypotheses hold if the two summands are respectively\n\\(O(2^{-\\eta N})\\) and \\(O(2^{-\\gamma N/2})\\), for arbitrary \\(\\eta,\\gamma&gt;0\\). No rate of convergence of \\(\\alpha_N\\) to \\(\\alpha_\\infty\\) is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\vartheta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\), equal to one on \\([-1,1]\\) and supported in \\([-2,2]\\). Let \\(Q_N\\) be convolution with the inverse Fourier transform of \\(\\vartheta(2^{-N}r)\\), and put \\(P_N=Q_N-Q_{N-1}\\). Its multiplier is supported in\n\\[\n\\{2^{N-1}\\le|r|\\le2^{N+1}\\}.\n\\]\nDilation of the Schwartz kernel gives\n\\[\n\\|Q_N\\nu\\|_{L^1}\\le C_\\vartheta\\|\\nu\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_{N=a}^bP_N\\nu\\right\\|_{L^1}\n \\le2C_\\vartheta\\|\\nu\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}.\n\\]\nThe second inequality is telescoping, independent of the number of bands.</p><p>Plancherel and bounded multipliers give\n\\[\n\\|P_N\\tau_N\\|_{L^2}\n \\lesssim\n \\left(\\int_{2^{N-1}\\le|r|\\le2^{N+1}}\n               |\\widehat{\\tau_N}(r)|^2\\,dr\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nG=\\sum_{N\\ge K}P_N\\tau_N\n\\quad\\text{converges absolutely in }L^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nH=\\sum_{N\\ge K}P_N(\\alpha_N-\\tau_N)\n\\quad\\text{converges absolutely in }L^1.\n\\]</p><p>Set \\(\\zeta_n=\\alpha_n-\\alpha_{n+1}\\ge0\\). Monotonicity gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\ge K}\\|\\zeta_n\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n =\\alpha_K(\\mathbb R)-\\alpha_\\infty(\\mathbb R)&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_N-\\alpha_\\infty=\\sum_{n\\ge N}\\zeta_n\n\\quad\\text{in total variation}.\n\\]\nFor finite \\(M\\), define\n\\[\nZ_M=\\sum_{N=K}^MP_N(\\alpha_N-\\alpha_\\infty).\n\\]\nSubstitute the total-variation-convergent increment series into this finite sum:\n\\[\nZ_M=\\sum_{n\\ge K}(Q_{\\min(n,M)}-Q_{K-1})\\zeta_n.\n\\]\nThis series converges in \\(L^1\\). Also\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_{n\\ge K}(Q_n-Q_{K-1})\\zeta_n\n\\]\nconverges absolutely in \\(L^1\\), and\n\\[\n\\|Z_M-Z\\|_{L^1}\n \\le2C_\\vartheta\\sum_{n&gt;M}\\|\\zeta_n\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n =2C_\\vartheta\\|\\alpha_{M+1}-\\alpha_\\infty\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n \\to0.\n\\]\nThis is the use of monotonicity; no unjustified double-series rearrangement occurs.</p><p>Finite telescoping now gives\n\\[\nQ_M\\alpha_\\infty\n =Q_{K-1}\\alpha_\\infty\n  +\\sum_{N=K}^MP_N\\tau_N\n  +\\sum_{N=K}^MP_N(\\alpha_N-\\tau_N)-Z_M.\n\\]\nThe right side converges in \\(L^1+L^2\\), hence in distributions, to\n\\[\nf=Q_{K-1}\\alpha_\\infty+G+H-Z\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R).\n\\]\nThe left side converges in distributions to \\(\\alpha_\\infty\\), since \\(Q_M\\) is an approximate identity. Therefore\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\infty=f\\,\\mathcal L^1.\n\\]\nPositivity and finiteness imply \\(f\\ge0\\) almost everywhere and \\(f\\in L^1\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [4, 1, "part"], [5, 1, "part"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [9, 6, "part"], [10, 6, "part"], [11, 6, "part"], [12, 6, "part"], [15, 14, "part"], [16, 14, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [20, 17, "part"], [21, 17, "part"], [22, 17, "part"], [25, 24, "part"], [26, 24, "part"], [27, 24, "part"], [28, 24, "part"], [29, 24, "part"], [31, 30, "part"], [32, 30, "part"], [33, 30, "part"], [35, 34, "part"], [36, 34, "part"], [38, 37, "part"], [40, 39, "part"], [41, 39, "part"], [43, 42, "part"], [44, 42, "part"]]});
